WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.015 --> 00:00:10.917
Theo: Hi, I'm Theo from UIT.

2
00:00:10.917 --> 00:00:16.086
Theo: Welcome back to UIT INSIDE, the podcast for
developers who love user interfaces and technology.

3
00:00:16.086 --> 00:00:22.755
Theo: Today I'm joined by ubugeeei and baseballyama

4
00:00:22.755 --> 00:00:30.146
Theo: for an episode to get us excited about Vue Fes
2026.

5
00:00:30.146 --> 00:00:31.368
Theo: Thanks for joining me.

6
00:00:31.909 --> 00:00:33.271
ubugeeei / baseballyama: Thanks for having us.

7
00:00:33.271 --> 00:00:36.916
Theo: Let's start with some introductions, shall we?

8
00:00:37.197 --> 00:00:41.223
ubugeeei: Sure, I'll go first. I'm ubugeeei.

9
00:00:41.223 --> 00:00:47.633
ubugeeei: I think this is my third time on UIT INSIDE.

10
00:00:47.633 --> 00:00:51.960
ubugeeei: As for what I do, I'm a Vue.js core team
member.

11
00:00:52.045 --> 00:00:56.853
ubugeeei: I build various tools and write a lot of Vue
at work.

12
00:00:56.853 --> 00:01:01.882
ubugeeei: I'm also on the Vue Fes core staff.

13
00:01:01.882 --> 00:01:04.046
Theo: Are you speaking at this year's Vue Fes too?

14
00:01:03.947 --> 00:01:10.498
ubugeeei: Yes, I'm giving a talk as a guest speaker. We
might get into this later,

15
00:01:10.498 --> 00:01:13.823
ubugeeei: but apparently there's a chibivue hands-on
workshop this year,

16
00:01:13.823 --> 00:01:16.948
ubugeeei: so I'm planning to drop by that as well.

17
00:01:16.948 --> 00:01:19.994
Theo: Is chibivue a community?

18
00:01:19.994 --> 00:01:26.284
ubugeeei: Well, it's, you know... [unclear] Something
we'll all do together.

19
00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:25.950
Theo: Oh, that sounds fun.

20
00:01:26.384 --> 00:01:29.629
ubugeeei: Definitely. Although there aren't any tickets
left; it's sold out.

21
00:01:30.315 --> 00:01:32.000
Theo: Yes, if you managed to get a ticket.

22
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.604
ubugeeei: Yes, I hope to see you there.

23
00:01:34.604 --> 00:01:36.147
Theo: And next, Yama.

24
00:01:36.529 --> 00:01:42.463
baseballyama: Hi, I'm Yamashita. I work at a company
called flyle,

25
00:01:42.463 --> 00:01:45.872
baseballyama: where my title is VP of Technology.

26
00:01:45.872 --> 00:01:49.559
baseballyama: I'm on the Svelte core team.

27
00:01:49.559 --> 00:01:54.488
baseballyama: Officially, I mainly maintain things like
the ESLint plugin,

28
00:01:54.986 --> 00:02:00.156
baseballyama: but lately I've also been working on a
project

29
00:02:00.156 --> 00:02:05.487
baseballyama: to try writing a Svelte compiler and
formatter in Rust.

30
00:02:05.487 --> 00:02:06.609
baseballyama: Thanks for having me.

31
00:02:07.511 --> 00:02:14.967
Theo: Thanks for coming. Writing compilers and linters
in Rust

32
00:02:14.967 --> 00:02:20.615
Theo: is actually our main topic today.

33
00:02:20.615 --> 00:02:26.202
Theo: Lately, a lot of Vue Fes talks seem to be about
building tools in Rust rather than JavaScript.

34
00:02:26.202 --> 00:02:33.851
Theo: And both of you have recently been working on

35
00:02:33.851 --> 00:02:38.256
Theo: some kind of Rust tooling for your respective
frameworks.

36
00:02:38.256 --> 00:02:41.600
Theo: In your case, ubugeeei, that's Vize?

37
00:02:41.779 --> 00:02:46.650
ubugeeei: Yes. I'm working on a project called Vize.

38
00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:52.323
ubugeeei: It's a Vue.js toolchain with a compiler, type
checker, formatter, linter,

39
00:02:52.323 --> 00:02:55.550
ubugeeei: and a bunch of other things, written in Rust.

40
00:02:56.882 --> 00:03:03.395
Theo: And Yama, you're writing a Svelte compiler and
formatter in Rust too.

41
00:03:03.415 --> 00:03:07.464
Theo: So here's an incredibly broad question: why?
[laughs]

42
00:03:07.065 --> 00:03:11.734
ubugeeei: Why? [laughs] Who wants to go first?

43
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:12.600
baseballyama: Oh, go ahead. [laughs]

44
00:03:11.914 --> 00:03:15.602
ubugeeei: To put it very broadly,

45
00:03:15.602 --> 00:03:19.611
ubugeeei: when you write Rust... well, if you put the
effort in,

46
00:03:19.611 --> 00:03:22.054
ubugeeei: and I'll stress that to avoid any
misunderstanding,

47
00:03:22.054 --> 00:03:26.481
ubugeeei: you can make it really fast. That's basically
why.

48
00:03:26.481 --> 00:03:29.785
ubugeeei: A lot of existing TypeScript toolchains

49
00:03:29.785 --> 00:03:37.766
ubugeeei: weren't designed around performance from the
outset.

50
00:03:37.766 --> 00:03:45.120
ubugeeei: With Rust, it's easier to really commit to
writing extremely fast code,

51
00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.847
ubugeeei: and the result actually is extremely fast.
That's my reason.

52
00:03:48.847 --> 00:03:50.350
Theo: Native performance.

53
00:03:50.251 --> 00:03:56.983
ubugeeei: Yes, that too. But the language also makes it
easier to optimize memory allocation

54
00:03:56.983 --> 00:04:01.992
ubugeeei: and computation at compile time. Compared with
TypeScript,

55
00:04:01.992 --> 00:04:04.757
ubugeeei: you have more room for low-level tuning.

56
00:04:04.757 --> 00:04:12.511
ubugeeei: The language's compiler itself is also very
good and very fast. That's another reason.

57
00:04:12.511 --> 00:04:13.893
ubugeeei: Is it the same for you, Yama?

58
00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:21.771
baseballyama: Basically, yes. But let me explain it from
a slightly different angle.

59
00:04:21.771 --> 00:04:27.259
baseballyama: I maintain things like
eslint-plugin-svelte.

60
00:04:27.259 --> 00:04:33.108
baseballyama: I've always felt that linting Svelte files
takes quite a while,

61
00:04:33.108 --> 00:04:36.307
baseballyama: and we've had issues filed about it too.

62
00:04:36.307 --> 00:04:42.302
baseballyama: When we tried Oxlint on a large project at
work, it was incredibly fast.

63
00:04:42.302 --> 00:04:46.673
baseballyama: But it couldn't natively support Svelte
files,

64
00:04:46.673 --> 00:04:49.681
baseballyama: so we still needed eslint-plugin-svelte.

65
00:04:49.681 --> 00:04:56.971
baseballyama: It felt like such a waste, given how fast
Oxlint was. That's one reason.

66
00:04:56.971 --> 00:05:04.162
baseballyama: And now that AI can write so much code,
things have changed. Before, you'd work hard on an
implementation,

67
00:05:04.162 --> 00:05:08.348
baseballyama: then run the linter while you went to get
a coffee.

68
00:05:08.348 --> 00:05:13.099
baseballyama: That was fine. But now, implementation
happens in parallel,

69
00:05:13.099 --> 00:05:18.531
baseballyama: so shortening that feedback loop has a
very direct impact on development speed.

70
00:05:18.531 --> 00:05:22.861
baseballyama: As that changes, I thought it'd be good
for Svelte to join the move to Rust,

71
00:05:22.861 --> 00:05:26.830
baseballyama: so I decided to give it a try. That's the
background.

72
00:05:27.431 --> 00:05:32.721
Theo: What you said about AI really resonates with me.

73
00:05:33.082 --> 00:05:40.637
Theo: For personal projects, I use an M1 MacBook Air
with 8 GB of RAM.

74
00:05:41.319 --> 00:05:46.749
Theo: It's a really underpowered machine. [laughs] If I
vibe-code in parallel on it,

75
00:05:47.312 --> 00:05:54.203
Theo: it starts filling up swap almost immediately. It's
painful.

76
00:05:54.203 --> 00:05:59.692
Theo: If the formatter, compiler, and linter all run
natively,

77
00:05:59.692 --> 00:06:04.781
Theo: they can use memory more efficiently too.

78
00:06:04.781 --> 00:06:12.163
Theo: That's definitely becoming an important
consideration.

79
00:06:12.163 --> 00:06:15.790
Theo: But as you touched on earlier, over the past one,
two,

80
00:06:15.790 --> 00:06:22.842
Theo: or three years, there's been this movement to take
the toolchain native

81
00:06:22.842 --> 00:06:25.366
Theo: within the JavaScript ecosystem.

82
00:06:25.366 --> 00:06:32.718
Theo: We've got Oxlint, Oxc, and TypeScript 7's tsgo,
for example.

83
00:06:32.718 --> 00:06:38.749
Theo: So why do we still need implementations
specifically for Svelte and Vue?

84
00:06:39.410 --> 00:06:44.660
Theo: I'm asking that very casually, but I suspect the
answer is pretty complicated. [laughs]

85
00:06:45.311 --> 00:06:50.757
baseballyama: Well, the short answer is that a Svelte
file isn't JavaScript

86
00:06:50.757 --> 00:06:55.262
baseballyama: or TypeScript. That's the starting point.
A Svelte file,

87
00:06:55.262 --> 00:06:58.245
baseballyama: and I think Vue is the same here,

88
00:06:58.245 --> 00:07:06.154
baseballyama: gets roughly split into template, script,
and style sections, using things like regular
expressions.

89
00:07:06.154 --> 00:07:10.439
baseballyama: Then a parser for each language runs on
those sections.

90
00:07:10.629 --> 00:07:18.208
baseballyama: Those three parts are related, so you
analyze how they fit together,

91
00:07:18.208 --> 00:07:20.614
baseballyama: and then ultimately compile the result,

92
00:07:20.614 --> 00:07:25.407
baseballyama: or use it for linting, formatting, and so
on. Very roughly,

93
00:07:25.407 --> 00:07:27.129
baseballyama: that's how the process works.

94
00:07:27.129 --> 00:07:31.897
baseballyama: Toolchains like Oxc understand JavaScript
and TypeScript.

95
00:07:31.897 --> 00:07:36.625
baseballyama: I think they're starting to support some
HTML and other languages too,

96
00:07:36.625 --> 00:07:41.994
baseballyama: but they can't inherently understand
framework-specific languages

97
00:07:41.994 --> 00:07:49.607
baseballyama: like Svelte or Vue. You need some way to
teach them that.

98
00:07:49.607 --> 00:07:55.839
baseballyama: With Oxc as it stands, you go through
JavaScript to call a parser or whatever you need,

99
00:07:55.839 --> 00:08:01.869
baseballyama: and eventually make it work. The ecosystem
is built around using

100
00:08:01.869 --> 00:08:04.193
baseballyama: the existing JavaScript ecosystem.

101
00:08:04.193 --> 00:08:08.741
baseballyama: But then you drop back into JavaScript and
lose performance there.

102
00:08:08.741 --> 00:08:12.988
baseballyama: And, getting into the details,

103
00:08:13.191 --> 00:08:17.980
baseballyama: you have to exchange all sorts of data
between Rust and JavaScript.

104
00:08:17.980 --> 00:08:23.309
baseballyama: Even if one side gets faster, the overhead
of that exchange can wipe out the benefit.

105
00:08:23.309 --> 00:08:26.535
baseballyama: That's roughly where things stand, I
think.

106
00:08:28.389 --> 00:08:33.399
ubugeeei: Also, talking about Oxc and Vite+,

107
00:08:33.660 --> 00:08:40.474
ubugeeei: they're aiming to be very framework-agnostic.

108
00:08:40.474 --> 00:08:44.322
ubugeeei: They don't want to lean toward a particular
framework.

109
00:08:44.322 --> 00:08:48.230
ubugeeei: They're building a more general JavaScript and
web toolchain.

110
00:08:48.791 --> 00:08:56.748
ubugeeei: So making Vue and Svelte built into Oxc

111
00:08:57.470 --> 00:09:01.939
ubugeeei: would probably go against that philosophy,
although there are some Vue rules.

112
00:09:01.939 --> 00:09:06.970
ubugeeei: There are tradeoffs there too.

113
00:09:08.173 --> 00:09:14.665
ubugeeei: Even for an integrated toolchain, bundling
every framework in the world would make it huge.

114
00:09:14.665 --> 00:09:22.280
ubugeeei: It'd be hard to implement, and above all,
require an enormous amount of developer time.

115
00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:27.270
ubugeeei: So there's a bit of a division of
responsibilities. I'm building the Vue toolchain,

116
00:09:27.492 --> 00:09:33.682
ubugeeei: and people actually joke that the Oxc team
can't allocate resources to Vue because I'm already
doing it. [laughs]

117
00:09:33.682 --> 00:09:40.193
ubugeeei: That's how short of people the ecosystem is
overall.

118
00:09:40.193 --> 00:09:45.882
ubugeeei: So we're taking the approach of building
something in the community, as a third-party project,

119
00:09:45.882 --> 00:09:50.330
ubugeeei: that can integrate well with those tools.

120
00:09:52.671 --> 00:10:00.083
baseballyama: I think this is a tricky issue. We're
getting into the details now,

121
00:10:00.083 --> 00:10:04.530
baseballyama: but there's something I've wanted to ask
you about, ubugeeei.

122
00:10:04.550 --> 00:10:08.456
baseballyama: As long as Rust and JavaScript are both
involved in the current way,

123
00:10:08.456 --> 00:10:11.160
baseballyama: making Svelte and Vue really fast,

124
00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:14.606
baseballyama: in the way we're imagining, just isn't
possible.

125
00:10:14.606 --> 00:10:17.150
baseballyama: I think we can take that as our starting
assumption.

126
00:10:17.511 --> 00:10:24.503
baseballyama: So we need Rust, or at least some faster
approach. It doesn't have to be Rust.

127
00:10:25.285 --> 00:10:31.416
baseballyama: But Oxc, for example, is a natively
compiled executable,

128
00:10:31.416 --> 00:10:36.685
baseballyama: and it doesn't include the ability to
understand Svelte or Vue files.

129
00:10:36.685 --> 00:10:41.691
baseballyama: Somehow, you have to give it that ability.

130
00:10:41.691 --> 00:10:46.877
baseballyama: You could use dynamic calls, or whatever
the right term is.

131
00:10:46.877 --> 00:10:51.503
baseballyama: But even if you defined an ABI for that,

132
00:10:51.503 --> 00:10:56.910
baseballyama: Oxlint, for instance, already has a fixed
set of AST node types.

133
00:10:56.910 --> 00:11:02.698
baseballyama: It can't understand Svelte-specific nodes,
so I don't think it could process them correctly.

134
00:11:02.698 --> 00:11:05.962
baseballyama: To integrate that support natively,

135
00:11:05.962 --> 00:11:13.911
baseballyama: it seems like Oxc's core, or a layer close
to it, would ultimately need some knowledge

136
00:11:13.911 --> 00:11:16.935
baseballyama: of Svelte and Vue nodes. Otherwise, I
don't see how it could work.

137
00:11:16.935 --> 00:11:23.770
baseballyama: Which makes me feel we'd have to get code
that understands Svelte and Vue

138
00:11:23.770 --> 00:11:27.896
baseballyama: into Oxc itself. There was an RFC in Oxc
about this,

139
00:11:27.896 --> 00:11:32.181
baseballyama: but I understood it as: first convert
Svelte or Vue into JavaScript,

140
00:11:32.181 --> 00:11:35.186
baseballyama: then figure out how to handle it from
there.

141
00:11:35.186 --> 00:11:38.490
baseballyama: But that loses all the framework-specific
semantics.

142
00:11:38.871 --> 00:11:44.378
baseballyama: We're putting all this effort into
building Vize and rsvelte,

143
00:11:44.378 --> 00:11:51.248
baseballyama: and I'm really struggling with what the
path forward should be.

144
00:11:51.248 --> 00:11:55.815
baseballyama: I wanted to ask how you're thinking about
that.

145
00:11:55.815 --> 00:11:59.162
ubugeeei: We've gotten pretty deep. Sorry.

146
00:11:59.162 --> 00:12:03.427
Theo: Before we go there,

147
00:12:03.427 --> 00:12:10.077
Theo: let me check whether I, as the non-expert here,
actually understand. [laughs]

148
00:12:10.077 --> 00:12:17.448
Theo: Oxc is a toolkit for building things like
JavaScript ASTs, abstract syntax trees.

149
00:12:17.448 --> 00:12:23.420
Theo: Then Oxlint and Oxfmt run on top of it to do
formatting

150
00:12:23.420 --> 00:12:31.237
Theo: and linting. But it's fundamentally a JavaScript
toolkit,

151
00:12:31.237 --> 00:12:37.430
Theo: so you can't directly apply it to supersets like
Vue or Svelte.

152
00:12:37.771 --> 00:12:45.504
Theo: Their templates contain JavaScript inside
something that looks like HTML,

153
00:12:45.504 --> 00:12:50.031
Theo: which means those tools can't use them directly.

154
00:12:50.031 --> 00:12:57.303
Theo: Oxc doesn't include an HTML parser, so it has no
way to understand them. Is that right?

155
00:12:57.704 --> 00:13:05.077
Theo: And from the Oxc team's perspective, if you want
to process Svelte or Vue,

156
00:13:05.077 --> 00:13:12.970
Theo: you should first use a framework-specific tool,
like Vize or rsvelte,

157
00:13:12.970 --> 00:13:20.081
Theo: to turn the template into JavaScript, or something
resembling JavaScript,

158
00:13:20.081 --> 00:13:25.290
Theo: and pass that to Oxc. That's the proposed design.

159
00:13:25.911 --> 00:13:31.979
Theo: And you're saying that would undermine the
performance gains from going native?

160
00:13:31.979 --> 00:13:36.325
Theo: Is that the concern?

161
00:13:36.325 --> 00:13:38.528
baseballyama: I agree, apart from that last bit.

162
00:13:38.528 --> 00:13:42.835
baseballyama: I'd like ubugeeei to confirm whether we're
right. But on the last point,

163
00:13:43.936 --> 00:13:48.823
baseballyama: if converting Svelte or Vue files to
JavaScript really did solve everything,

164
00:13:49.232 --> 00:13:54.143
baseballyama: then as long as that conversion could
happen through a native-to-native dynamic call,

165
00:13:54.143 --> 00:13:58.553
baseballyama: I think we'd get the full performance
benefit.

166
00:13:58.553 --> 00:14:05.710
baseballyama: The problem is that converting to
JavaScript loses Svelte- and Vue-specific information.

167
00:14:05.710 --> 00:14:09.998
baseballyama: With ESLint, let has Svelte-specific
implications, especially in Svelte 4 and earlier.

168
00:14:09.998 --> 00:14:19.315
baseballyama: That kind of thing

169
00:14:19.315 --> 00:14:22.260
baseballyama: would be ignored, so linting wouldn't be
correct.

170
00:14:22.260 --> 00:14:25.806
baseballyama: Since all that framework-specific
information gets lost,

171
00:14:25.806 --> 00:14:30.314
baseballyama: I don't think that approach is really
viable. It's a problem before you even get to
performance.

172
00:14:30.433 --> 00:14:34.543
Theo: So the framework changes how JavaScript itself is
interpreted,

173
00:14:34.543 --> 00:14:38.373
Theo: and Oxc's general-purpose understanding of
JavaScript isn't enough.

174
00:14:38.373 --> 00:14:41.000
baseballyama: That's how I see it.

175
00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:45.432
Theo: That was my layperson's paraphrase. What do you
think, ubugeeei?

176
00:14:47.710 --> 00:14:55.005
ubugeeei: Right, there are several points here. To give
our predecessors their due,

177
00:14:55.005 --> 00:15:01.578
ubugeeei: we've been talking about Oxc,

178
00:15:01.578 --> 00:15:07.270
ubugeeei: but SWC was doing JavaScript tooling in Rust
before that.

179
00:15:07.512 --> 00:15:13.746
ubugeeei: SWC handles some of this through things like
dynamic linking. With SWC,

180
00:15:13.746 --> 00:15:21.844
ubugeeei: you write the plugins

181
00:15:21.844 --> 00:15:27.479
ubugeeei: in Rust. But its users are JavaScript
developers,

182
00:15:27.479 --> 00:15:31.466
ubugeeei: so they want to write plugins in JavaScript.

183
00:15:31.466 --> 00:15:39.461
ubugeeei: Oxc has made choices to balance those
tradeoffs, including compatibility with existing
JavaScript plugins,

184
00:15:39.461 --> 00:15:44.530
ubugeeei: and I think some of that is unavoidable.

185
00:15:45.811 --> 00:15:52.221
ubugeeei: Not everyone can write native plugins,

186
00:15:52.221 --> 00:15:58.712
ubugeeei: so we'll leave that point there for now.
[laughs] Also,

187
00:15:58.712 --> 00:16:06.705
ubugeeei: on the question of whether Oxc itself needs to
understand

188
00:16:06.705 --> 00:16:09.910
ubugeeei: framework-specific behavior for this to work,

189
00:16:10.799 --> 00:16:16.331
ubugeeei: and all that discussion about ABIs,

190
00:16:16.331 --> 00:16:23.286
ubugeeei: I do sometimes talk to Evan You about Vite and
Oxc.

191
00:16:23.286 --> 00:16:31.259
ubugeeei: The short answer is that, right now,

192
00:16:31.259 --> 00:16:36.550
ubugeeei: there probably isn't a definitive solution.
For conflicting rules, you just disable the built-in
ones

193
00:16:36.550 --> 00:16:44.247
ubugeeei: and handle them on the Vue or framework side.
With more AI-assisted coding now,

194
00:16:44.247 --> 00:16:49.697
ubugeeei: it's feasible to implement those ourselves, so
I don't see it as a huge problem.

195
00:16:49.697 --> 00:16:56.368
ubugeeei: There's waste, of course. Something already
exists upstream, but a small difference means we can't
use it.

196
00:16:56.368 --> 00:17:03.700
ubugeeei: Still, I'm fairly optimistic: we can build our
own version and make it work.

197
00:17:03.700 --> 00:17:08.000
ubugeeei: That's one point. There are a couple more. Is
it okay to keep going?

198
00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:10.812
Theo: Please go on.

199
00:17:10.812 --> 00:17:14.999
ubugeeei: This is very much cutting-edge stuff.

200
00:17:14.999 --> 00:17:19.569
ubugeeei: It hasn't been settled globally, let alone
released,

201
00:17:19.569 --> 00:17:25.017
ubugeeei: but TSGo has an initiative called Content
Mappers.

202
00:17:25.017 --> 00:17:33.591
ubugeeei: And today is September 25; just this week,

203
00:17:33.591 --> 00:17:41.604
ubugeeei: someone on the Rolldown team personally
proposed an RFC to change

204
00:17:41.604 --> 00:17:45.142
ubugeeei: the API for Vite's transform pipeline.

205
00:17:45.142 --> 00:17:47.946
ubugeeei: It's precisely about cases like Vue.

206
00:17:47.946 --> 00:17:53.936
ubugeeei: If you read the RFC, you'll see Vue used as an
example.

207
00:17:53.936 --> 00:18:01.689
ubugeeei: Vite's compilation pipeline is currently just
a chain of transforms.

208
00:18:02.493 --> 00:18:08.441
ubugeeei: There's no way to tell Vite or the rest of the
ecosystem

209
00:18:08.441 --> 00:18:13.068
ubugeeei: how the JavaScript in that pipeline was
actually produced.

210
00:18:13.068 --> 00:18:19.639
ubugeeei: The RFC proposes making that declarative and
composable.

211
00:18:19.639 --> 00:18:26.930
ubugeeei: I don't think Vite or Rolldown will ever
interpret Vue or Svelte themselves,

212
00:18:26.930 --> 00:18:34.721
ubugeeei: but they could work with more abstract
information about where this JavaScript came from:

213
00:18:34.721 --> 00:18:39.647
ubugeeei: process this internally, leave that alone, or
let Vite's build handle it.

214
00:18:39.647 --> 00:18:47.518
ubugeeei: That kind of work is happening, and I think
it'll be pretty central to Vize,

215
00:18:47.518 --> 00:18:52.285
ubugeeei: at least. Of course, that's a discussion about
Vite and Rolldown.

216
00:18:52.285 --> 00:18:57.938
ubugeeei: For linters and formatters, it's still early
days.

217
00:18:57.938 --> 00:19:03.169
ubugeeei: A lot remains undecided. As for TypeScript
integration,

218
00:19:04.132 --> 00:19:09.962
ubugeeei: Content Mappers is being implemented precisely
for things like Svelte and Vue.

219
00:19:10.102 --> 00:19:15.752
ubugeeei: I think it'll come in the next minor release,
probably sometime after October or November,

220
00:19:15.752 --> 00:19:19.338
ubugeeei: so it isn't out yet.

221
00:19:19.338 --> 00:19:23.645
ubugeeei: It operates at a similar level to Vite's
transformers.

222
00:19:24.335 --> 00:19:31.948
ubugeeei: Rather than understanding Vue or Svelte
themselves, it provides an API to declare

223
00:19:32.088 --> 00:19:39.282
ubugeeei: what the TypeScript generated as an
intermediate representation actually means.

224
00:19:39.282 --> 00:19:43.670
ubugeeei: So it still stays framework-agnostic and
neutral,

225
00:19:43.670 --> 00:19:51.773
ubugeeei: but moves beyond the existing APIs to express
where the JavaScript or TypeScript came from,

226
00:19:51.773 --> 00:19:57.970
ubugeeei: and to design APIs that account for that in
the first place.

227
00:19:57.970 --> 00:20:02.260
ubugeeei: I'm watching how that develops.

228
00:20:02.260 --> 00:20:08.833
ubugeeei: That's roughly where things stand for me.

229
00:20:08.833 --> 00:20:12.039
ubugeeei: I wonder if any of that came across. [laughs]

230
00:20:12.039 --> 00:20:17.310
baseballyama: So you're saying we should first separate
type checking, Vite bundling, linting,

231
00:20:17.310 --> 00:20:22.159
baseballyama: and formatting. Treat them as different
problems.

232
00:20:22.159 --> 00:20:26.747
baseballyama: I'm really looking forward to Content
Mappers for types too.

233
00:20:26.747 --> 00:20:31.496
baseballyama: Once 7.1 comes out, I think we can move
everything at work to TSGo.

234
00:20:31.496 --> 00:20:36.000
baseballyama: I believe svelte-check already has a PR
for Content Mappers support,

235
00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:38.007
Theo: It's currently blocked, right?

236
00:20:38.007 --> 00:20:39.810
baseballyama: Yes, that's what's blocking it.

237
00:20:39.831 --> 00:20:46.767
baseballyama: Once that lands, all our Svelte projects
at work can finally move to TSGo.

238
00:20:46.767 --> 00:20:51.919
baseballyama: That's pretty great. And I feel similarly
about Vite.

239
00:20:52.019 --> 00:20:56.670
baseballyama: Every time, you end up transforming
everything back into source code.

240
00:20:57.470 --> 00:21:05.021
baseballyama: You've already built an AST, so why parse
it all over again? [laughs] It's always felt wasteful.

241
00:21:05.021 --> 00:21:09.867
baseballyama: I hadn't heard about the RFC from this
week,

242
00:21:09.867 --> 00:21:11.950
baseballyama: but that sounds like a great initiative.

243
00:21:12.151 --> 00:21:17.157
baseballyama: If we could then call that natively,
without going through JavaScript at all,

244
00:21:17.157 --> 00:21:22.624
baseballyama: that'd be excellent. But then there's
linting. [laughs]

245
00:21:22.624 --> 00:21:30.513
baseballyama: With your Vize... Vize, right. With Vize,

246
00:21:30.513 --> 00:21:37.441
baseballyama: and that little oxlint-plugins project
we've been working on together,

247
00:21:37.441 --> 00:21:41.185
baseballyama: which does sort of exist,

248
00:21:41.185 --> 00:21:45.930
baseballyama: you first parse and check everything with
Oxlint, and that's one pass done.

249
00:21:45.930 --> 00:21:51.361
baseballyama: Then another plugin parses and checks
everything again. Oxlint itself is so fast, [laughs]

250
00:21:51.361 --> 00:21:56.030
baseballyama: so it's fine in a sense, but personally I
still find it very inefficient.

251
00:21:56.030 --> 00:22:01.300
baseballyama: It'd be nice if Oxlint had a slightly more
elegant solution

252
00:22:01.300 --> 00:22:07.253
baseballyama: for that. And then there's formatting.

253
00:22:07.253 --> 00:22:10.017
baseballyama: You didn't really mention formatters
earlier,

254
00:22:11.340 --> 00:22:17.388
baseballyama: but they're already much faster than
linters,

255
00:22:17.388 --> 00:22:20.493
baseballyama: so they're probably a somewhat lower
priority.

256
00:22:20.493 --> 00:22:27.138
baseballyama: Vize and rsvelte currently take a similar
approach: wrapping Oxfmt

257
00:22:27.138 --> 00:22:33.715
baseballyama: and providing it that way. I think that's
fine for the time being.

258
00:22:33.715 --> 00:22:39.910
ubugeeei: This gets into some fairly experimental work
in Vize,

259
00:22:39.910 --> 00:22:47.515
ubugeeei: but we've been talking about ASTs, or
JavaScript and TypeScript as intermediate
representations,

260
00:22:47.656 --> 00:22:52.070
ubugeeei: as the interface to other, existing
toolchains.

261
00:22:52.070 --> 00:22:59.119
ubugeeei: And about repeatedly manipulating ASTs or
running them separately through a type checker or
formatter.

262
00:22:59.119 --> 00:23:04.625
ubugeeei: Well, lately, Vize has actually been moving
toward

263
00:23:04.625 --> 00:23:07.769
ubugeeei: getting rid of the AST. [laughs]

264
00:23:09.492 --> 00:23:13.216
Theo: How deep does this rabbit hole go? [laughs]

265
00:23:16.617 --> 00:23:24.609
ubugeeei: For people familiar with the terminology, I'm
trying to build something like a Vue.js version of MLIR.

266
00:23:24.609 --> 00:23:28.535
ubugeeei: That's what I'm working toward.

267
00:23:28.535 --> 00:23:36.609
ubugeeei: It's a more abstract intermediate
representation. It's neither an AST nor a CST,

268
00:23:36.609 --> 00:23:40.418
ubugeeei: nor a red-green tree like Biome's.

269
00:23:40.880 --> 00:23:44.830
ubugeeei: It captures Vue's semantics and structure,

270
00:23:44.830 --> 00:23:52.530
ubugeeei: including the styling information needed by
the formatter, across multiple layers of IR.

271
00:23:52.631 --> 00:23:56.800
ubugeeei: I'm trying to unify things through that.

272
00:23:56.800 --> 00:24:04.756
ubugeeei: It doesn't solve the fact that separate
processing still happens on the Oxc side,

273
00:24:04.756 --> 00:24:06.981
ubugeeei: but for the formatter, linter,

274
00:24:06.981 --> 00:24:11.430
ubugeeei: and type checker, I want to eliminate
redundant work in the Vue-specific parts.

275
00:24:12.290 --> 00:24:15.338
ubugeeei: That's something I've been working on lately.

276
00:24:15.338 --> 00:24:21.473
ubugeeei: Repeatedly parsing an AST, or keeping a
separate AST for the formatter,

277
00:24:21.473 --> 00:24:27.468
ubugeeei: are things I'm trying hard to eliminate.

278
00:24:27.468 --> 00:24:29.312
Theo: This is getting very compiler-like...

279
00:24:29.398 --> 00:24:32.622
ubugeeei: It really is a compiler discussion.

280
00:24:32.622 --> 00:24:40.052
Theo: Rather than directly converting the AST into
JavaScript,

281
00:24:40.052 --> 00:24:48.042
Theo: you create an abstract, Vue-specific
representation to work with.

282
00:24:48.042 --> 00:24:54.150
Theo: Then processing it that way avoids all the
repeated back-and-forth?

283
00:24:54.303 --> 00:24:58.150
ubugeeei: Just to clarify a little,

284
00:24:58.150 --> 00:25:02.438
ubugeeei: I'm not putting every piece of information
into one intermediate representation.

285
00:25:02.438 --> 00:25:07.709
ubugeeei: MLIR has intermediate representations at
different layers. There's a concept called lowering,

286
00:25:07.709 --> 00:25:15.504
ubugeeei: where you go down to the next representation.
So there's a layer needed for formatting,

287
00:25:15.504 --> 00:25:20.024
ubugeeei: one for semantic analysis, one for type
checking,

288
00:25:20.024 --> 00:25:28.022
ubugeeei: and, experimentally, layers for incorporating
languages other than JavaScript as dialects.

289
00:25:28.022 --> 00:25:36.021
ubugeeei: With this multilayer IR, I want to unify the
tools needed for the Vue-specific parts.

290
00:25:36.041 --> 00:25:43.456
ubugeeei: After that, Rolldown or Oxlint will probably
take over,

291
00:25:43.456 --> 00:25:48.827
ubugeeei: with some adjustments on our side, such as
disabling built-in behavior we can't use and handling it
ourselves.

292
00:25:48.827 --> 00:25:56.701
ubugeeei: That's broadly how I'm trying to improve
performance and cut out redundant work.

293
00:25:56.701 --> 00:26:02.610
ubugeeei: I'm still building it, but that's the idea.

294
00:26:02.610 --> 00:26:09.301
Theo: So you're trying to resolve these issues within
Vize's intermediate representations,

295
00:26:09.301 --> 00:26:14.970
Theo: without waiting for a general solution from the
JavaScript toolchain.

296
00:26:14.970 --> 00:26:15.270
ubugeeei: Yes.

297
00:26:16.371 --> 00:26:24.151
baseballyama: In that flow, do you start with a Vue file
and parse it just once,

298
00:26:24.151 --> 00:26:31.049
baseballyama: then call the formatter and linter within
that same process and finish everything there?

299
00:26:31.049 --> 00:26:31.349
baseballyama: Right?

300
00:26:31.459 --> 00:26:39.237
ubugeeei: No, it's a bit different. It's less like an
existing pipeline.

301
00:26:39.237 --> 00:26:47.177
ubugeeei: It's more ad hoc, and even the structure of
the optimization passes is quite different.

302
00:26:47.177 --> 00:26:51.513
ubugeeei: You don't start by converting an AST into an
IR.

303
00:26:51.513 --> 00:26:55.178
ubugeeei: There are choices about which approach makes
more sense.

304
00:26:55.178 --> 00:27:00.424
ubugeeei: For instance, parsing expressions comes up in
various places, right?

305
00:27:00.424 --> 00:27:07.994
ubugeeei: Before you've parsed everything, you bring in
the semantics of Vue's syntax as you reach those points.

306
00:27:08.191 --> 00:27:14.361
ubugeeei: When you encounter a binding, for example,
that part gets parsed as a JavaScript expression.

307
00:27:14.361 --> 00:27:19.268
ubugeeei: It's quite interconnected. It isn't really a
case of first doing Vue,

308
00:27:19.268 --> 00:27:22.733
ubugeeei: then JavaScript. You move back and forth,

309
00:27:23.234 --> 00:27:27.962
ubugeeei: so you have to thread the needle carefully.
[laughs]

310
00:27:27.962 --> 00:27:33.210
Theo: Will you cover this in your Vue Fes talk?

311
00:27:33.572 --> 00:27:36.497
ubugeeei: I'm planning to touch on it,

312
00:27:36.497 --> 00:27:44.454
ubugeeei: but lots of people at Vue Fes probably won't
have heard of Vize,

313
00:27:44.454 --> 00:27:47.600
ubugeeei: so it'll be a more general talk,

314
00:27:47.600 --> 00:27:52.330
ubugeeei: and I can't go that deep in 30 minutes, so I
won't cover it in detail.

315
00:27:52.330 --> 00:27:56.077
ubugeeei: But I'll mention that this is the sort of
thing I'm working on.

316
00:27:56.077 --> 00:28:00.344
ubugeeei: There's still about a month to go,

317
00:28:00.344 --> 00:28:06.053
ubugeeei: so I hope to share the progress too. But I
won't spend the whole talk focusing on that.

318
00:28:05.654 --> 00:28:10.800
Theo: By the way, Vapor was announced at Vue Fes two
years ago.

319
00:28:10.800 --> 00:28:15.467
Theo: There was some discussion of the middle end then
too,

320
00:28:15.467 --> 00:28:18.452
Theo: and something like MLIR,

321
00:28:18.452 --> 00:28:20.655
Theo: an inter... [trails off]

322
00:28:20.655 --> 00:28:26.804
ubugeeei: MLIR itself is part of the LLVM project.

323
00:28:26.804 --> 00:28:31.270
ubugeeei: IR stands for intermediate representation.
It's the representation in between.

324
00:28:31.833 --> 00:28:34.518
Theo: Intermediate representation. Right.

325
00:28:34.518 --> 00:28:38.085
Theo: That's entirely down to Theo's Japanese.

326
00:28:38.085 --> 00:28:40.290
Theo: So there's an intermediate representation.

327
00:28:40.290 --> 00:28:47.844
Theo: Is the IR used by the Vapor compiler the same as
the one Vize uses internally?

328
00:28:47.825 --> 00:28:51.772
ubugeeei: Good question. That's a really good question.

329
00:28:51.772 --> 00:28:59.405
ubugeeei: I want to unify that on the Vize side too.
Vize has its own compiler,

330
00:28:59.405 --> 00:29:02.409
ubugeeei: and Vapor mode currently has an IR,

331
00:29:02.530 --> 00:29:09.137
ubugeeei: but upstream Vue.js doesn't have that concept
for the regular VNode-based mode.

332
00:29:09.137 --> 00:29:12.761
ubugeeei: When you think about it, that's a little
awkward, isn't it?

333
00:29:12.761 --> 00:29:15.665
ubugeeei: A common Vue.js IR with shared semantics would
be cleaner,

334
00:29:15.665 --> 00:29:22.772
ubugeeei: and then connect it neatly to both VNodes and
Vapor.

335
00:29:22.772 --> 00:29:25.395
ubugeeei: So my work encompasses that too.

336
00:29:25.864 --> 00:29:31.090
ubugeeei: If you're asking whether it's the same as
Vapor's IR, it'll actually be quite different.

337
00:29:31.090 --> 00:29:36.977
ubugeeei: But architecturally, it's closer to how
Vapor's current IR works.

338
00:29:36.977 --> 00:29:40.381
ubugeeei: I want to bring the VNode side onto that
approach too.

339
00:29:40.381 --> 00:29:47.710
Theo: One of LLVM's strengths is that different
languages use the same IR.

340
00:29:47.732 --> 00:29:53.864
Theo: That made me wonder whether all frontend
frameworks might someday share an IR.

341
00:29:55.327 --> 00:29:58.213
Theo: Does rsvelte use an IR internally too?

342
00:29:58.213 --> 00:30:02.281
baseballyama: No, rsvelte doesn't do anything quite that
complicated.

343
00:30:03.043 --> 00:30:05.648
baseballyama: Our thinking differs a little there.

344
00:30:06.003 --> 00:30:13.934
baseballyama: My understanding is that Vize tries more
advanced ideas

345
00:30:13.934 --> 00:30:17.459
baseballyama: and gives the good results back to the
community.

346
00:30:17.459 --> 00:30:25.451
baseballyama: rsvelte has a simpler scope: take the
Svelte ecosystem

347
00:30:25.451 --> 00:30:28.055
baseballyama: and replace its implementation with Rust.

348
00:30:29.280 --> 00:30:32.527
baseballyama: Partly, that's because I can't come up
with ideas that advanced,

349
00:30:32.527 --> 00:30:39.844
baseballyama: but I also want it to be practical, and
that requires solid compatibility,

350
00:30:39.844 --> 00:30:42.550
baseballyama: especially with upstream.

351
00:30:42.550 --> 00:30:47.725
baseballyama: That's where I'm putting more of my
effort.

352
00:30:47.725 --> 00:30:55.301
baseballyama: I've heard Vue people discuss this IR idea
a few times,

353
00:30:55.301 --> 00:30:58.227
baseballyama: and it'd be amazing if it worked.

354
00:30:58.227 --> 00:31:02.155
ubugeeei: I'm trying to make it happen. [laughs] Just
putting that out there quietly.

355
00:31:03.372 --> 00:31:08.400
Theo: With an IR, you'd need a complete representation,
wouldn't you?

356
00:31:08.400 --> 00:31:16.393
Theo: You'd need syntax and concepts that can express
anything

357
00:31:16.393 --> 00:31:21.460
Theo: found in any frontend framework. But some things
exist only in Vue, not Svelte,

358
00:31:21.460 --> 00:31:27.109
Theo: and plenty exist in Svelte but not Vue. How do you
handle that?

359
00:31:28.231 --> 00:31:33.261
ubugeeei: That's exactly the idea behind the MLIR
approach I mentioned.

360
00:31:33.261 --> 00:31:37.691
ubugeeei: As I said, there isn't just one IR.

361
00:31:37.691 --> 00:31:42.520
ubugeeei: MLIR stands for Multi-Level Intermediate
Representation.

362
00:31:44.188 --> 00:31:51.120
ubugeeei: It has this concept of dialects, which can
take care of Vue-specific things.

363
00:31:51.120 --> 00:31:56.770
ubugeeei: So it's a shared, multilayer IR.

364
00:31:56.770 --> 00:32:01.017
ubugeeei: In Vize, I'm building something like a
UI-level IR.

365
00:32:01.017 --> 00:32:06.066
ubugeeei: Whether it's Svelte, Vue, or Angular, you have
bindings,

366
00:32:06.548 --> 00:32:11.236
ubugeeei: derived state, and so on. That's the more
abstract layer.

367
00:32:11.236 --> 00:32:17.808
ubugeeei: Then the design also accepts concepts specific
to Vue or Svelte as dialects.

368
00:32:17.808 --> 00:32:19.832
ubugeeei: I haven't built the Svelte part yet,

369
00:32:19.832 --> 00:32:23.539
ubugeeei: and I wouldn't actually put Svelte support
into Vize,

370
00:32:23.539 --> 00:32:28.288
ubugeeei: but I want it to be possible to connect to
that.

371
00:32:28.288 --> 00:32:35.954
ubugeeei: Once you lower things to this IR, the
remaining compilation could emit similar JavaScript.

372
00:32:35.954 --> 00:32:40.538
ubugeeei: That's precisely what I'm aiming for right
now. Well, aiming for...

373
00:32:40.538 --> 00:32:46.204
ubugeeei: This is largely a hobby project. It simply
sounds interesting, so I want to try it.

374
00:32:46.204 --> 00:32:51.350
ubugeeei: That's really where I'm coming from. Another
thing I've been thinking about lately

375
00:32:54.171 --> 00:33:00.985
ubugeeei: is preprocessors. Some projects use them.
Maybe they're less common now,

376
00:33:00.985 --> 00:33:04.231
ubugeeei: but older projects may still want them.

377
00:33:04.231 --> 00:33:11.165
ubugeeei: For templates, there's Pug, for example, or
Sass and SCSS for styles.

378
00:33:11.165 --> 00:33:15.787
ubugeeei: Vue actually lets you write templates in Pug
by setting lang="pug",

379
00:33:15.787 --> 00:33:20.274
ubugeeei: but that sort of thing isn't supported either.

380
00:33:20.274 --> 00:33:24.420
ubugeeei: A multilayer IR would help with those cases
too.

381
00:33:24.420 --> 00:33:29.067
ubugeeei: Whether you write Pug or the regular syntax,
you could have an IR layer

382
00:33:29.067 --> 00:33:36.893
ubugeeei: that represents the same meaning. With those
multiple layers,

383
00:33:36.893 --> 00:33:42.923
ubugeeei: I'm trying to normalize all of that. That's
another thing I've been working on.

384
00:33:42.824 --> 00:33:48.572
Theo: Comparing these two projects, I'm seeing a pretty
big difference.

385
00:33:48.572 --> 00:33:53.440
Theo: There's a more general solution

386
00:33:53.440 --> 00:33:59.729
Theo: and a more directly applied one. Though Vize is
practical too, of course.

387
00:33:59.729 --> 00:34:07.562
Theo: But over the long term, something like that IR

388
00:34:07.562 --> 00:34:10.006
Theo: gives it a different ultimate goal.

389
00:34:11.808 --> 00:34:14.973
baseballyama: Another reason I'm not pursuing that
approach,

390
00:34:15.974 --> 00:34:21.362
baseballyama: and there's no single right answer here;
people should explore what interests them and let good
ideas spread,

391
00:34:24.682 --> 00:34:32.131
baseballyama: is that more layers mean more abstractions
and more things to think about. It gets very difficult.

392
00:34:32.131 --> 00:34:35.515
baseballyama: Implementation itself has become
incredibly fast now.

393
00:34:35.515 --> 00:34:42.763
baseballyama: If you want to create a UI framework, you
can just create it without thinking about all those
abstractions.

394
00:34:42.763 --> 00:34:46.027
baseballyama: Define the syntax, write a compiler to
turn it into JavaScript,

395
00:34:46.027 --> 00:34:48.210
baseballyama: then write a runtime, and there you go.

396
00:34:48.630 --> 00:34:53.776
baseballyama: You can just build one straightforward
pipeline without all that abstraction, and you're done.

397
00:34:53.776 --> 00:35:00.222
baseballyama: The biggest reason is that other people
are already thinking about those problems,

398
00:35:00.222 --> 00:35:05.448
baseballyama: but personally, I also think there's
something to be said for whoever has a good idea

399
00:35:05.448 --> 00:35:09.412
baseballyama: just building a straightforward pipeline
for it. That's simpler.

400
00:35:09.412 --> 00:35:13.636
ubugeeei: DHH! DHH! DHH! [laughs]

401
00:35:14.997 --> 00:35:20.544
baseballyama: [laughs] Right. Rust is the best language,
as long as you don't read it.

402
00:35:20.544 --> 00:35:23.207
ubugeeei: We used to talk constantly about things like
DRY...

403
00:35:23.247 --> 00:35:29.474
ubugeeei: Oh, I see. Now AI can handle all of that at
once, so it isn't such a problem anymore.

404
00:35:29.496 --> 00:35:32.603
ubugeeei: Yeah. You can just write each one separately.

405
00:35:32.663 --> 00:35:36.031
baseballyama: Ah, that's the idea.

406
00:35:36.031 --> 00:35:42.869
Theo: That's the thing that was going viral on X,
formerly Twitter, this morning. [laughs]

407
00:35:42.869 --> 00:35:47.897
Theo: Vibe coding is turning code into something
disposable,

408
00:35:47.897 --> 00:35:53.205
Theo: so maybe we don't even need to think about IRs
anymore.

409
00:35:53.205 --> 00:35:58.193
Theo: Just have AI write it directly. That's the idea,
right?

410
00:35:58.193 --> 00:35:59.695
ubugeeei: If it scales horizontally.

411
00:35:59.972 --> 00:36:06.082
Theo: Right. I'd like to dig into something here.

412
00:36:06.082 --> 00:36:11.752
Theo: I'm curious about the response from the community
and other maintainers.

413
00:36:11.752 --> 00:36:19.126
Theo: I imagine not every Vue or Svelte maintainer can
write Rust.

414
00:36:19.126 --> 00:36:21.450
Theo: How have people reacted?

415
00:36:24.953 --> 00:36:30.621
ubugeeei: Well, for context, Evan You, the creator of
Vue.js, can't write Rust.

416
00:36:30.621 --> 00:36:34.608
ubugeeei: People at VoidZero can, but Evan himself
hasn't written Rust.

417
00:36:37.072 --> 00:36:42.783
ubugeeei: At one point, he even moved from preferring
TypeScript to preferring JavaScript. [laughs]

418
00:36:42.783 --> 00:36:47.733
ubugeeei: That's a little like Rich Harris, Svelte's
creator. [laughs]

419
00:36:47.733 --> 00:36:51.060
ubugeeei: As for the core team,

420
00:36:51.060 --> 00:36:55.369
ubugeeei: among the people currently active

421
00:36:55.369 --> 00:37:02.358
ubugeeei: in Vue's core team, most probably can't write
Rust.

422
00:37:02.358 --> 00:37:05.161
ubugeeei: After I started writing Rust,

423
00:37:07.109 --> 00:37:14.359
ubugeeei: there have been some people who've said
they've started too,

424
00:37:14.359 --> 00:37:20.789
ubugeeei: but on the whole, people don't write it much.

425
00:37:20.789 --> 00:37:24.014
ubugeeei: And it's not just a Rust issue.

426
00:37:24.014 --> 00:37:27.480
ubugeeei: The people building JavaScript toolchains, at
SWC, VoidZero, or Oxc,

427
00:37:27.480 --> 00:37:34.477
ubugeeei: often don't know much about Vue or Svelte
either.

428
00:37:34.477 --> 00:37:41.836
ubugeeei: There aren't many specialists in that overlap.
It's because we sit between those worlds

429
00:37:41.836 --> 00:37:48.064
ubugeeei: that Yama and I are doing this. I think that's
a more accurate way to put it.

430
00:37:48.064 --> 00:37:55.495
ubugeeei: I guess there aren't that many Rust developers
on the Svelte team either?

431
00:37:55.495 --> 00:37:59.101
baseballyama: I haven't heard anyone say they're really
fluent in Rust.

432
00:37:59.101 --> 00:38:04.930
Theo: Do you discuss rsvelte in the maintainers'
channel?

433
00:38:04.510 --> 00:38:12.504
baseballyama: Yes. First, there are all kinds of people
on Svelte's core team,

434
00:38:12.504 --> 00:38:15.609
baseballyama: so this isn't a unanimous position. But
from reading the channel,

435
00:38:15.978 --> 00:38:23.948
baseballyama: there seems to be a broad consensus that
we'll have to get good at using AI.

436
00:38:23.948 --> 00:38:28.033
baseballyama: That's the direction things seem to be
heading.

437
00:38:28.033 --> 00:38:34.863
baseballyama: And of course, everyone agrees that Oxc
and Rust-based toolchains are very fast.

438
00:38:34.863 --> 00:38:40.530
baseballyama: Given that, if we can write the toolchain
in Rust, then of course that sounds better.

439
00:38:40.530 --> 00:38:47.980
baseballyama: A consensus along those lines may
gradually be forming, with Rich Harris at the center of
it.

440
00:38:47.980 --> 00:38:55.250
baseballyama: That's where we are. The problem is that
many people aren't fluent in Rust.

441
00:38:55.250 --> 00:39:00.757
baseballyama: When you want to add a major new feature
to Svelte, like async Svelte,

442
00:39:00.757 --> 00:39:08.573
baseballyama: and you're not fluent in Rust, with AI
doing most of the coding,

443
00:39:09.014 --> 00:39:12.118
baseballyama: how do you actually make implementation
work?

444
00:39:12.118 --> 00:39:14.821
baseballyama: That's still a major challenge.

445
00:39:14.821 --> 00:39:21.490
baseballyama: We've discussed how moving Rust to the
center of things would be difficult without solving
that.

446
00:39:21.490 --> 00:39:26.016
baseballyama: We're considering whether to put it on the
agenda

447
00:39:26.016 --> 00:39:32.125
baseballyama: for the offsite meeting before Svelte
Summit.

448
00:39:32.125 --> 00:39:37.293
baseballyama: The Svelte project is also working on
getting better at using AI.

449
00:39:37.293 --> 00:39:44.665
baseballyama: In some Svelte repositories, AI has
recently been triaging bugs and opening PRs.

450
00:39:44.665 --> 00:39:47.829
baseballyama: Sometimes the changes are wrong and get
reverted,

451
00:39:48.272 --> 00:39:53.162
baseballyama: but my impression lately is that people
are putting a lot of effort into this.

452
00:39:53.162 --> 00:39:55.567
ubugeeei: Another thing I keep coming back to

453
00:39:55.567 --> 00:40:01.460
ubugeeei: is that people say they can't write Rust
because they haven't written it before.

454
00:40:01.460 --> 00:40:06.205
ubugeeei: If Rust becomes the language we use, I think
people will end up writing it, because they'll have to.

455
00:40:06.205 --> 00:40:10.570
ubugeeei: I'm fairly optimistic about that.

456
00:40:10.570 --> 00:40:15.656
ubugeeei: Evan founded VoidZero, a company building
JavaScript tooling in Rust,

457
00:40:15.656 --> 00:40:20.756
ubugeeei: without being able to write Rust himself. But
he can make a small fix if he needs to,

458
00:40:20.756 --> 00:40:28.753
ubugeeei: and people who can write it can do that work.
You don't say, 'Our team only knows TypeScript,

459
00:40:28.753 --> 00:40:34.947
ubugeeei: so we'll only use TypeScript.' To put it
bluntly, programming languages are only tools.

460
00:40:35.188 --> 00:40:44.795
ubugeeei: If everyone decides Rust is a good fit and the
project moves to Rust, people will find ways to write
it.

461
00:40:44.795 --> 00:40:50.270
ubugeeei: And when it comes to whether a language is
easy or hard for the team to use,

462
00:40:50.270 --> 00:40:57.204
ubugeeei: it wasn't as though TypeScript

463
00:40:59.428 --> 00:41:03.175
ubugeeei: was selected because the core team happened to
have lots of people

464
00:41:03.175 --> 00:41:06.120
ubugeeei: who were extremely good at it.

465
00:41:06.341 --> 00:41:11.249
ubugeeei: We call it a core team, but a lot of the work
really comes down to individuals.

466
00:41:11.391 --> 00:41:19.142
ubugeeei: It's less like a company setting priorities
and more like, if someone can do the work, why not?

467
00:41:19.142 --> 00:41:26.313
ubugeeei: I'm fairly optimistic about that. Take
Vue.js's core team.

468
00:41:26.313 --> 00:41:28.737
ubugeeei: It sounds like a big team,

469
00:41:28.737 --> 00:41:33.565
ubugeeei: but the Vapor compiler is effectively being
driven by one person on the core team.

470
00:41:33.565 --> 00:41:38.257
ubugeeei: Edison is doing an enormous amount of that
work. That's how these things go, right?

471
00:41:38.257 --> 00:41:40.902
ubugeeei: Others aren't necessarily working on those
parts.

472
00:41:41.483 --> 00:41:48.175
ubugeeei: If there's one person who's really good at
Rust, I think it's fine for things to revolve around
Rust.

473
00:41:48.175 --> 00:41:50.479
ubugeeei: Of course, that depends on the team's
direction.

474
00:41:50.479 --> 00:41:58.013
ubugeeei: Lately, I haven't really felt that 'nobody
knows Rust' is ultimately such a big concern.

475
00:41:58.414 --> 00:42:02.824
ubugeeei: At VoidZero,

476
00:42:02.824 --> 00:42:07.973
ubugeeei: the Vite team in particular weren't originally
Rust developers.

477
00:42:07.973 --> 00:42:15.966
ubugeeei: With Rolldown and Vite, they've had to reach
into Rust,

478
00:42:15.966 --> 00:42:20.874
ubugeeei: and now they're doing substantial Rust work in
Rolldown. That's actually happening.

479
00:42:20.874 --> 00:42:26.163
ubugeeei: I think something similar could happen here.

480
00:42:26.163 --> 00:42:32.000
baseballyama: Interesting.

481
00:42:32.000 --> 00:42:33.875
Theo: We've gone in a slightly different direction

482
00:42:33.875 --> 00:42:41.107
Theo: than I expected. Something just occurred to me.

483
00:42:41.107 --> 00:42:46.828
Theo: This is more abstract, maybe even philosophical,

484
00:42:46.828 --> 00:42:54.368
Theo: but both Vize and rsvelte are being built more or
less by one person, or one person plus AI.

485
00:42:54.511 --> 00:43:01.741
Theo: That's what the contributions look like, anyway.
In the future of open source,

486
00:43:01.741 --> 00:43:09.191
Theo: will we see more projects built by a single person
like that?

487
00:43:09.191 --> 00:43:16.882
Theo: Or, to ask a pretty big question, will open-source
communities still be necessary?

488
00:43:16.882 --> 00:43:24.033
Theo: I'm curious how you think about that.

489
00:43:24.033 --> 00:43:31.821
baseballyama: Well, to give my answer first, I think
communities

490
00:43:31.821 --> 00:43:35.044
baseballyama: are absolutely necessary. The reason is,

491
00:43:35.304 --> 00:43:40.910
baseballyama: to make something better, you need to hear
from its users.

492
00:43:41.411 --> 00:43:46.520
baseballyama: Something that works well for you might be
enough for version 1,

493
00:43:46.520 --> 00:43:53.572
baseballyama: but if you want to grow it into version 2
or 3, you need to know about different use cases.

494
00:43:53.572 --> 00:43:56.517
baseballyama: Otherwise, you can't make something better
or more useful.

495
00:43:56.517 --> 00:43:59.843
baseballyama: I think the only way to get that is
through community feedback.

496
00:43:59.843 --> 00:44:04.170
baseballyama: What matters is how people actually want
to use it.

497
00:44:04.511 --> 00:44:09.622
baseballyama: So I don't think that changes: communities
will remain absolutely necessary.

498
00:44:09.622 --> 00:44:13.430
baseballyama: That's how I see it.

499
00:44:13.430 --> 00:44:19.944
baseballyama: As for whether we'll see more impressive
open-source projects built by one person,

500
00:44:19.944 --> 00:44:22.670
baseballyama: I think that's largely how it's always
been.

501
00:44:22.670 --> 00:44:30.424
baseballyama: Someone extraordinarily productive
suddenly builds something amazing. Anthony Fu is like
that.

502
00:44:30.424 --> 00:44:35.915
baseballyama: Take Git, for example. Linus Torvalds
suddenly created it,

503
00:44:35.915 --> 00:44:39.706
baseballyama: and it came together incredibly quickly,
in about ten days, didn't it?

504
00:44:39.706 --> 00:44:43.257
ubugeeei: Evan and Rich Harris are similar.

505
00:44:43.257 --> 00:44:44.301
baseballyama: Vite too, right?

506
00:44:44.301 --> 00:44:50.689
baseballyama: Evan had a good idea one evening, coded
until morning, and had the first version.

507
00:44:50.689 --> 00:44:57.998
baseballyama: An extraordinarily productive person with
a good idea builds something,

508
00:44:57.998 --> 00:45:02.844
baseballyama: and it spreads because it's good. I think
that's always been the pattern.

509
00:45:02.844 --> 00:45:06.908
baseballyama: In that sense, I don't think the
underlying structure changes much.

510
00:45:06.908 --> 00:45:10.617
baseballyama: One thing that may change is that someone
can have a great idea,

511
00:45:10.617 --> 00:45:17.272
baseballyama: but be too busy with work, family, or
other commitments to put time into it.

512
00:45:17.272 --> 00:45:20.058
baseballyama: It's easier now for them to make those
ideas real.

513
00:45:20.996 --> 00:45:24.521
baseballyama: That could let more good things make it
out into the world.

514
00:45:24.521 --> 00:45:29.388
baseballyama: That's one way to look at it.

515
00:45:29.388 --> 00:45:31.852
baseballyama: This might be a little off topic,

516
00:45:31.852 --> 00:45:37.160
baseballyama: but what worries me about AI coding, at
least as of 2026, is quality.

517
00:45:37.160 --> 00:45:40.845
baseballyama: With code written by highly productive
people,

518
00:45:40.845 --> 00:45:45.454
baseballyama: you sometimes hear that people who write
code quickly somehow also produce fewer bugs.

519
00:45:45.574 --> 00:45:51.827
baseballyama: But even now, in 2026, AI can introduce
terrible bugs in places you didn't expect.

520
00:45:51.827 --> 00:45:58.120
baseballyama: So I think we could use more established
practices for maintaining quality,

521
00:45:58.120 --> 00:46:05.273
baseballyama: and it would be good for those practices
to become more standardized.

522
00:46:05.273 --> 00:46:10.680
baseballyama: That would make it easier to feel
confident using projects written with AI.

523
00:46:10.680 --> 00:46:13.624
baseballyama: That's something I think about.

524
00:46:13.624 --> 00:46:20.533
ubugeeei: I completely agree, especially that this is
largely how it's always been.

525
00:46:20.533 --> 00:46:25.540
ubugeeei: Evan built Vue mostly on his own,

526
00:46:25.540 --> 00:46:28.000
ubugeeei: and of course Rich created Svelte.

527
00:46:28.000 --> 00:46:34.563
baseballyama: And Rollup too. That was Rich as well.
[laughs]

528
00:46:34.563 --> 00:46:41.574
ubugeeei: So in that sense,

529
00:46:41.574 --> 00:46:49.566
ubugeeei: it's still the same story: exceptionally
capable people becoming even more capable.

530
00:46:49.566 --> 00:46:55.882
ubugeeei: Setting that aside, you also mentioned wanting
more ways

531
00:46:55.882 --> 00:46:59.388
ubugeeei: to maintain quality.

532
00:46:59.388 --> 00:47:05.378
ubugeeei: With Vize, for instance, we have far more test
cases and fixtures than people used to write by hand.

533
00:47:07.261 --> 00:47:10.412
ubugeeei: We pull huge numbers of them from GitHub and
run tons of tests.

534
00:47:10.412 --> 00:47:16.266
ubugeeei: Another thing I've been doing lately,

535
00:47:16.266 --> 00:47:23.925
ubugeeei: in most of my open-source projects, is writing
an 'ubugeeei-redundancy' document to make my own role
replaceable.

536
00:47:23.925 --> 00:47:31.817
ubugeeei: The idea is that, given that document, an AI
could do roughly the same work I do.

537
00:47:31.817 --> 00:47:39.667
ubugeeei: That's the kind of documentation it is. And
this may sound very irresponsible,

538
00:47:39.667 --> 00:47:45.453
ubugeeei: but it's an unofficial third-party project; it
isn't being merged into the main project.

539
00:47:45.453 --> 00:47:51.140
ubugeeei: If there's a problem, people can fork it and
use their own version.

540
00:47:51.140 --> 00:47:55.882
ubugeeei: I've put it under the MIT license to allow
that too.

541
00:47:55.882 --> 00:48:02.578
ubugeeei: Honestly, I don't currently see the person
rapidly building it becoming a single point of failure

542
00:48:02.578 --> 00:48:05.925
ubugeeei: as such a major problem here.

543
00:48:05.925 --> 00:48:12.938
ubugeeei: There are lots of tests, there's
documentation, and it isn't going straight into
upstream.

544
00:48:12.938 --> 00:48:20.306
ubugeeei: So I'm just carrying on with it in that
spirit.

545
00:48:20.306 --> 00:48:23.389
Theo: What does adoption look like? Do Vize and rsvelte

546
00:48:23.389 --> 00:48:25.892
Theo: have many users yet?

547
00:48:29.883 --> 00:48:32.486
baseballyama: Vize probably has more.

548
00:48:32.486 --> 00:48:37.352
baseballyama: rsvelte is used in a meta-framework called
Mochi, for example.

549
00:48:37.393 --> 00:48:42.319
baseballyama: And occasionally people seem to be using
it, because we get issues from them.

550
00:48:42.319 --> 00:48:45.704
baseballyama: I don't really know how much it's being
used in production,

551
00:48:45.704 --> 00:48:50.992
baseballyama: but if the question is whether anyone is
using it at all, there do seem to be a few users.

552
00:48:50.992 --> 00:48:52.053
baseballyama: Yeah.

553
00:48:52.296 --> 00:48:56.342
Theo: Can you use it with SvelteKit, for example?

554
00:48:56.342 --> 00:48:57.925
baseballyama: Yes, it works with SvelteKit.

555
00:48:57.925 --> 00:49:02.912
baseballyama: rsvelte has its own vite-plugin-svelte.

556
00:49:02.912 --> 00:49:08.100
baseballyama: If you use that, I think changing a single
line is enough to swap out the compiler.

557
00:49:08.100 --> 00:49:12.747
Theo: That compatibility you mentioned earlier. It's a
port rather than a rewrite.

558
00:49:12.747 --> 00:49:16.533
baseballyama: Right, it's a port of the Svelte compiler.

559
00:49:16.448 --> 00:49:19.073
Theo: And is Vize getting fairly widespread use?

560
00:49:19.073 --> 00:49:24.725
ubugeeei: No, it hasn't spread to the point of being
people's first choice.

561
00:49:24.725 --> 00:49:30.999
ubugeeei: But it does have actual users. And an
interesting thing

562
00:49:30.999 --> 00:49:36.170
ubugeeei: is that, by my rough impression, about half
the usage is through forks.

563
00:49:36.392 --> 00:49:43.168
ubugeeei: We're used to toolchains being distributed
through npm,

564
00:49:43.168 --> 00:49:47.338
ubugeeei: but Vize is still experimental, and naturally
some parts don't work for people yet.

565
00:49:47.338 --> 00:49:52.270
ubugeeei: Now it's much easier to fork it and change
some behavior, even if you can't read Rust.

566
00:49:52.270 --> 00:49:56.696
ubugeeei: I find that interesting, and it feels a little
new.

567
00:49:56.696 --> 00:50:03.585
ubugeeei: Either way, people use it as an npm package or
through forks.

568
00:50:03.585 --> 00:50:08.491
ubugeeei: The linter, compiler, and type checker all
seem to be getting use.

569
00:50:08.491 --> 00:50:16.053
ubugeeei: Some companies use it, and we use parts of it
at my own company too.

570
00:50:16.053 --> 00:50:16.353
ubugeeei: Yeah.

571
00:50:16.154 --> 00:50:21.631
baseballyama: Unlike JavaScript, you can't just make a
little tweak with pnpm patch. [laughs]

572
00:50:21.631 --> 00:50:25.796
baseballyama: If you really want to change something,
you have to fork it and build it yourself.

573
00:50:25.917 --> 00:50:27.058
ubugeeei: Exactly.

574
00:50:27.058 --> 00:50:28.880
Theo: That's just Rust being a pain. [laughs]

575
00:50:28.880 --> 00:50:30.823
baseballyama: That part is a bit of a hassle, isn't it?

576
00:50:30.983 --> 00:50:34.147
ubugeeei: It is, yeah.

577
00:50:34.147 --> 00:50:40.916
Theo: By the way, had both of you been writing Rust
before these projects?

578
00:50:40.838 --> 00:50:48.838
ubugeeei: I'd been writing quite a bit. Rust was one of
my favorite languages

579
00:50:50.062 --> 00:50:57.455
ubugeeei: even before the Oxc wave. I'd wanted to do
something in Rust

580
00:50:57.455 --> 00:51:03.541
ubugeeei: around Vue since about 2022 or 2023.

581
00:51:03.541 --> 00:51:08.065
ubugeeei: Then Oxc and similar tools started developing.

582
00:51:08.065 --> 00:51:11.548
ubugeeei: At that point,

583
00:51:11.548 --> 00:51:17.453
ubugeeei: AI wasn't nearly as wild as it is now,

584
00:51:17.453 --> 00:51:21.056
ubugeeei: but the idea had been on my mind for a long
time.

585
00:51:21.056 --> 00:51:25.080
ubugeeei: AI advanced so much that, as Yama said, ideas
can become reality very quickly.

586
00:51:27.256 --> 00:51:33.927
ubugeeei: That improvement was so dramatic that I really
got going this year.

587
00:51:33.927 --> 00:51:40.317
ubugeeei: So the idea goes back a long way, and I'd been
writing Rust for quite a while before that.

588
00:51:40.317 --> 00:51:43.783
baseballyama: I hadn't written that much Rust.

589
00:51:43.783 --> 00:51:48.772
baseballyama: When Oxc came along and I wanted to use it
at work,

590
00:51:48.772 --> 00:51:54.141
baseballyama: I wanted to understand what kind of tool
it was.

591
00:51:54.141 --> 00:52:00.190
baseballyama: For me, submitting pull requests is the
best way to understand something.

592
00:52:00.511 --> 00:52:06.441
baseballyama: So I read the code and documentation and
submitted a few PRs.

593
00:52:06.441 --> 00:52:11.712
baseballyama: That wasn't quite my first experience with
Rust, but it was pretty close.

594
00:52:11.712 --> 00:52:18.561
baseballyama: That's basically the extent of my
experience.

595
00:52:18.561 --> 00:52:23.548
baseballyama: I'm very fluent in JavaScript, although
maybe I've forgotten how to write it because I use AI so
much now.

596
00:52:23.548 --> 00:52:25.531
baseballyama: I can write JavaScript reasonably
fluently,

597
00:52:25.531 --> 00:52:29.876
baseballyama: but I'm shakier in Rust. I still wrestle
with compiler errors.

598
00:52:30.458 --> 00:52:33.081
baseballyama: That's roughly where I am.

599
00:52:33.924 --> 00:52:39.974
ubugeeei: Even so, I spend more time writing Rust than
TypeScript now.

600
00:52:36.700 --> 00:52:38.200
baseballyama: Oh! [laughs]

601
00:52:40.014 --> 00:52:44.282
baseballyama: For me, it's 99% languages other than
Rust. [laughs]

602
00:52:44.282 --> 00:52:48.249
baseballyama: That's about where things stand, yeah.

603
00:52:48.249 --> 00:52:55.685
Theo: One last thing: you're building quite a bit of
Vize and rsvelte

604
00:52:55.685 --> 00:53:00.192
Theo: through vibe coding these days, right?

605
00:53:00.192 --> 00:53:03.517
ubugeeei: That's right. Vibe coding.

606
00:53:03.000 --> 00:53:05.150
Theo: Is that not the case for you, ubugeeei?

607
00:53:03.517 --> 00:53:05.440
baseballyama: For me, yes, it's vibe coding. [laughs]

608
00:53:05.440 --> 00:53:09.106
baseballyama: Vibe coding, using AI.

609
00:53:10.709 --> 00:53:14.877
ubugeeei: There are still quite a few things I want to
write myself.

610
00:53:14.877 --> 00:53:21.720
ubugeeei: I don't really feel like I'm just coding by
vibes. [laughs]

611
00:53:23.559 --> 00:53:30.350
Theo: Maybe 'vibe coding' wasn't quite the right term.

612
00:53:30.350 --> 00:53:37.983
Theo: I meant that AI writes most of the code, and the
author reviews it.

613
00:53:40.114 --> 00:53:44.225
ubugeeei: I do a lot of code reading, although I don't
review every change individually.

614
00:53:44.225 --> 00:53:49.721
ubugeeei: But honestly, the rest of the time I'm
working, and it's the same at work.

615
00:53:49.721 --> 00:53:52.810
ubugeeei: Other team members implement things,

616
00:53:53.532 --> 00:53:59.691
ubugeeei: and the lead doesn't inspect every tiny
detail. That structure hasn't changed.

617
00:53:59.691 --> 00:54:05.450
ubugeeei: For me, this hasn't gone beyond that. There's
probably a huge amount of code,

618
00:54:05.450 --> 00:54:12.248
ubugeeei: but I understand and review it to roughly the
extent I would if I were leading a team.

619
00:54:12.248 --> 00:54:17.422
ubugeeei: It isn't a case of handing everything over
blindly.

620
00:54:17.422 --> 00:54:21.409
ubugeeei: Really, it hasn't changed all that much.

621
00:54:21.549 --> 00:54:25.116
ubugeeei: Instead of paying people to write code,

622
00:54:25.116 --> 00:54:29.223
ubugeeei: I pay to have generative AI generate it.

623
00:54:29.223 --> 00:54:35.635
ubugeeei: That's about the difference. What was the
original question again?

624
00:54:35.635 --> 00:54:38.220
baseballyama: Something like, 'Are you vibe coding?'

625
00:54:40.554 --> 00:54:45.514
Theo: Vibe coding has become so... Well, even if you
don't call it vibe coding,

626
00:54:46.200 --> 00:54:50.567
Theo: we're entering an era where people use AI to write
code.

627
00:54:50.567 --> 00:54:56.477
Theo: So my question is, why do Vize and rsvelte,

628
00:54:56.477 --> 00:55:04.470
Theo: or even the higher-level Vue and Svelte
themselves, need to be in the middle?

629
00:55:04.470 --> 00:55:09.117
Theo: Why not just have AI write JavaScript directly?

630
00:55:09.117 --> 00:55:15.406
Theo: Depending on how good the AI is, that might
perform better in some cases.

631
00:55:15.406 --> 00:55:20.092
Theo: I think frameworks exist to make code easier for
people to read,

632
00:55:20.092 --> 00:55:25.340
Theo: but do we still need to keep that layer?

633
00:55:25.340 --> 00:55:26.161
ubugeeei: Tough! [laughs]

634
00:55:30.471 --> 00:55:34.419
ubugeeei: It is. One thing we probably have in common is
a bit of stubborn pride.

635
00:55:34.419 --> 00:55:37.967
ubugeeei: We like Vue or Svelte, so we build tools for
them.

636
00:55:38.689 --> 00:55:44.000
ubugeeei: That's one reason. Do you have anything to
add, Yama?

637
00:55:45.539 --> 00:55:48.062
baseballyama: By writing JavaScript directly, you mean

638
00:55:48.062 --> 00:55:54.531
baseballyama: doing things like document.appendChild,
right?

639
00:55:54.531 --> 00:55:56.254
baseballyama: Wouldn't that be unreadable?

640
00:55:56.254 --> 00:56:01.782
ubugeeei: The idea is that maybe it no longer needs to
be readable. With AI,

641
00:56:01.782 --> 00:56:07.710
ubugeeei: we, as people whose job is to build UIs, could
stop using frameworks

642
00:56:08.694 --> 00:56:13.202
ubugeeei: and just have it write good JavaScript. Until
now, we needed to be able to read

643
00:56:13.202 --> 00:56:14.404
ubugeeei: and write it ourselves,

644
00:56:14.404 --> 00:56:22.241
ubugeeei: but maybe AI can take over some of the work
that frameworks used to do for us.

645
00:56:22.241 --> 00:56:30.620
baseballyama: To me, that's a bit like saying we should
have AI write assembly or machine code directly.
[laughs]

646
00:56:30.620 --> 00:56:38.101
baseballyama: I'd first separate work from hobbies.

647
00:56:38.101 --> 00:56:45.561
baseballyama: At work, I'm ultimately responsible, so
the code has to be something I can read,

648
00:56:45.561 --> 00:56:51.610
baseballyama: or at least understand properly if I put
my mind to it.

649
00:56:51.610 --> 00:56:58.320
baseballyama: Given my abilities, if a huge production
codebase were all document.appendChild

650
00:56:58.320 --> 00:57:02.527
baseballyama: and things like Svelte's compiled output,
I honestly couldn't understand it.

651
00:57:02.527 --> 00:57:04.530
baseballyama: Then I couldn't take responsibility for
it.

652
00:57:04.671 --> 00:57:09.680
baseballyama: So it needs to be written in a more
human-friendly, readable language.

653
00:57:09.680 --> 00:57:13.627
baseballyama: That's how I feel about work, at least.

654
00:57:13.627 --> 00:57:17.774
baseballyama: For hobbies, sure, maybe that approach is
fine.

655
00:57:17.774 --> 00:57:21.301
baseballyama: But even hobbies differ. If all you need
is for it to run,

656
00:57:21.962 --> 00:57:26.330
baseballyama: if it's just a hobby app, then direct AI
generation is honestly fine.

657
00:57:26.330 --> 00:57:31.261
baseballyama: You wouldn't need a build step, and
deployment would probably be easy. That's convenient, so
why not?

658
00:57:31.261 --> 00:57:33.846
baseballyama: But if you want to keep maintaining it,

659
00:57:33.846 --> 00:57:39.639
baseballyama: or make it open source and have a wider
community use it and help it grow,

660
00:57:39.639 --> 00:57:44.168
baseballyama: at some point, quality starts to matter to
some extent.

661
00:57:44.471 --> 00:57:50.240
baseballyama: Even under an MIT license, I think you'd
feel some responsibility.

662
00:57:50.240 --> 00:57:56.450
baseballyama: If the code is completely unmaintainable,
you can't take responsibility, just as in the work
example.

663
00:57:56.450 --> 00:58:01.097
baseballyama: Personally, that makes me question whether
it's a viable project.

664
00:58:01.097 --> 00:58:03.922
baseballyama: So whether it's Svelte, Vue, React, or
something else,

665
00:58:03.922 --> 00:58:09.110
baseballyama: I think we'll still need to write things
in a form that's easy for humans to read.

666
00:58:09.812 --> 00:58:17.801
ubugeeei: There's that, of course. But even for AI, is
compiled JavaScript

667
00:58:17.801 --> 00:58:22.828
ubugeeei: actually easier to read? I don't think it is.

668
00:58:22.828 --> 00:58:29.056
ubugeeei: If it keeps generating minified .js files,
reading and understanding those

669
00:58:29.056 --> 00:58:34.527
ubugeeei: seems like it'd take an enormous amount of
time. It's hard for AI to read too,

670
00:58:34.527 --> 00:58:37.933
ubugeeei: just as it is for us. If anything, I think

671
00:58:37.933 --> 00:58:45.648
ubugeeei: the more fast, smart, deterministic,
feature-rich tools we have at a higher level,

672
00:58:45.648 --> 00:58:50.136
ubugeeei: the faster AI can write code. That's why I'm
building this.

673
00:58:50.136 --> 00:58:57.203
ubugeeei: At the very beginning, Yama mentioned that
faster verification cycles are better.

674
00:58:57.203 --> 00:59:04.238
ubugeeei: If that layer has lots of useful features,
that should benefit AI too when it's building something.

675
00:59:04.238 --> 00:59:09.630
ubugeeei: Of course, whether you prefer React or Vue is
partly a matter of taste,

676
00:59:10.212 --> 00:59:14.316
ubugeeei: but that's another consideration.

677
00:59:14.316 --> 00:59:19.902
ubugeeei: So I don't really think AI changes that very
much.

678
00:59:19.902 --> 00:59:24.768
ubugeeei: That's roughly how I see it.

679
00:59:24.768 --> 00:59:31.735
baseballyama: But we're supposed to get incredibly
powerful ASI, and humanity goes extinct in 2030, right?
[laughs]

680
00:59:32.728 --> 00:59:37.660
ubugeeei: Sounds great. [laughs] I'm really looking
forward to that. [laughs]

681
00:59:37.660 --> 00:59:43.955
baseballyama: If AI is really that powerful, then for
them, and yes, I just called AI 'them',

682
00:59:44.718 --> 00:59:48.000
baseballyama: this might be an incredibly trivial
discussion. [laughs]

683
00:59:48.000 --> 00:59:52.478
ubugeeei: With recent AI models,

684
00:59:52.478 --> 00:59:58.129
ubugeeei: I've seen tuning make them produce
intermediate output in language humans can't even
understand. [laughs]

685
00:59:58.129 --> 01:00:05.082
ubugeeei: I saw that on X recently. It's
incomprehensible. [laughs]

686
01:00:04.893 --> 01:00:11.549
Theo: Right. It's not as though every smart person
thinks at a low level either.

687
01:00:11.549 --> 01:00:19.448
Theo: We don't write every little throwaway program in
assembly.

688
01:00:19.448 --> 01:00:26.446
Theo: So a smart AI might also have less to think about
with a higher-level language,

689
01:00:26.446 --> 01:00:33.784
Theo: and be able to focus its attention on the things
that matter more.

690
01:00:33.784 --> 01:00:36.550
Theo: It might be surprisingly similar to humans in that
respect.

691
01:00:36.471 --> 01:00:40.939
ubugeeei: It's about scale and choosing the right thing
for the situation.

692
01:00:40.939 --> 01:00:44.645
ubugeeei: That's the same as it's always been, isn't it?

693
01:00:44.645 --> 01:00:48.833
ubugeeei: I don't know exactly how large a product Yama
had in mind earlier,

694
01:00:48.833 --> 01:00:53.020
ubugeeei: At that scale, plain JavaScript would be
painful. You'd want a framework.

695
01:00:53.020 --> 01:00:57.167
ubugeeei: That's certainly one case. If you're just
making a personal website,

696
01:00:57.167 --> 01:01:03.317
ubugeeei: of course, you might not need one. It's about
choosing appropriately and how far you want to scale,

697
01:01:03.317 --> 01:01:07.124
ubugeeei: and how much responsibility you need to take.

698
01:01:07.124 --> 01:01:11.270
ubugeeei: There isn't a single silver bullet. It
ultimately depends on the situation.

699
01:01:11.270 --> 01:01:18.001
ubugeeei: Maybe the conclusion is simply that it's good
for both approaches to develop. That's how I see it.

700
01:01:17.742 --> 01:01:19.886
baseballyama: By the way, to go back a little,

701
01:01:19.886 --> 01:01:26.480
baseballyama: to your earlier question about whether
we're vibe coding Vize and rsvelte,

702
01:01:26.480 --> 01:01:30.989
baseballyama: this relates to what we've just been
discussing. With rsvelte,

703
01:01:31.031 --> 01:01:35.719
baseballyama: I'm deliberately giving AI a broader
remit.

704
01:01:35.719 --> 01:01:38.965
baseballyama: As I said earlier, at work, I have to take
responsibility.

705
01:01:38.965 --> 01:01:43.813
baseballyama: Even when I delegate to AI, I think very
carefully about the design,

706
01:01:43.813 --> 01:01:46.578
baseballyama: and check whether the resulting code makes
structural sense.

707
01:01:46.578 --> 01:01:49.563
baseballyama: I pay especially close attention to
database migrations.

708
01:01:49.563 --> 01:01:53.690
baseballyama: Honestly, I can't say I review every line
of everything else,

709
01:01:54.331 --> 01:02:00.823
baseballyama: but I do everything I can to fulfill my
responsibilities at work.

710
01:02:00.823 --> 01:02:06.675
baseballyama: Still, AI keeps improving, so I'm honestly
unsure how much of that will remain necessary.

711
01:02:06.675 --> 01:02:10.823
baseballyama: It's entirely possible we're putting less
trust in AI than we need to.

712
01:02:10.823 --> 01:02:14.370
baseballyama: Finding the right balance is a pretty
difficult question.

713
01:02:14.370 --> 01:02:17.997
baseballyama: But I can't take too much risk at work;
I'm responsible.

714
01:02:17.997 --> 01:02:20.682
baseballyama: I can't just leave everything to AI.

715
01:02:20.682 --> 01:02:27.393
baseballyama: So one of the ideas behind rsvelte is to
try taking that risk there instead.

716
01:02:27.393 --> 01:02:30.819
baseballyama: This is a very crude oversimplification,
and not literally how it happened,

717
01:02:30.819 --> 01:02:35.848
baseballyama: but the initial request was basically, 'I
want to port Svelte's compiler to Rust.

718
01:02:35.848 --> 01:02:37.290
baseballyama: It needs to be compatible,

719
01:02:37.471 --> 01:02:41.777
baseballyama: so please use the same directory structure
as the Svelte compiler project

720
01:02:41.777 --> 01:02:48.407
baseballyama: and implement it along the same lines.' To
put it very extremely, that's what got us this far.

721
01:02:48.407 --> 01:02:52.634
baseballyama: We've now compared real Svelte files from
more than a hundred projects

722
01:02:52.634 --> 01:02:57.721
baseballyama: against the upstream compiler, and we're
at almost 100% compatibility.

723
01:02:58.703 --> 01:03:04.726
baseballyama: With that much compatibility, most
projects work, which is honestly quite reassuring.

724
01:03:04.726 --> 01:03:08.050
baseballyama: So we have something that works reasonably
well.

725
01:03:08.050 --> 01:03:11.815
baseballyama: As Svelte releases new versions, we're
keeping up with them in real time,

726
01:03:11.815 --> 01:03:15.519
baseballyama: and that's going reasonably well too.

727
01:03:15.519 --> 01:03:20.606
baseballyama: As a project left almost entirely to AI,

728
01:03:20.606 --> 01:03:24.170
baseballyama: it's producing results: we're getting
something that works and is usable.

729
01:03:24.170 --> 01:03:29.442
baseballyama: The next issue, going back to our earlier
discussion, is implementing major new features.

730
01:03:29.442 --> 01:03:34.312
baseballyama: How do you add a new feature to what is,
in a sense, mysterious code written by AI?

731
01:03:34.312 --> 01:03:39.904
baseballyama: And when several people work together, can
they really maintain it

732
01:03:39.904 --> 01:03:42.770
baseballyama: if nobody understands its internals?
That's the next challenge.

733
01:03:42.892 --> 01:03:47.359
baseballyama: If we were to move rsvelte into the Svelte
organization,

734
01:03:47.359 --> 01:03:51.706
baseballyama: I think we'd first need to examine the
internals and properly consider the design,

735
01:03:51.706 --> 01:03:53.630
baseballyama: do some serious refactoring,

736
01:03:53.630 --> 01:03:59.139
baseballyama: and make it reasonably readable for humans
before moving it over.

737
01:03:59.139 --> 01:04:05.290
baseballyama: But I also want to test how far we can
actually leave things to AI.

738
01:04:05.290 --> 01:04:11.741
baseballyama: That's why I'm deliberately leaning more
heavily into vibe coding with rsvelte.

739
01:04:11.741 --> 01:04:15.588
baseballyama: My stance differs a little from Vize's
there. [laughs]

740
01:04:15.190 --> 01:04:20.894
ubugeeei: We should have talked more about the process
too. [laughs]

741
01:04:20.894 --> 01:04:23.486
baseballyama: We still can. [laughs] There's no time
limit.

742
01:04:22.666 --> 01:04:23.066
baseballyama: [laughs]

743
01:04:22.667 --> 01:04:26.371
ubugeeei: Yeah, that's the thing. With AI,

744
01:04:26.371 --> 01:04:32.381
ubugeeei: though not just AI, there are quite different
approaches to replacing an existing toolchain.

745
01:04:32.381 --> 01:04:38.010
ubugeeei: Ports are popular now, almost file for file,
using AI, people, or deterministic tools.

746
01:04:39.533 --> 01:04:46.505
ubugeeei: So there's the approach of doing a port,

747
01:04:46.505 --> 01:04:52.478
ubugeeei: and there's simply building something
yourself. How did Vize get started?

748
01:04:52.478 --> 01:05:00.171
ubugeeei: Actually, it was neither of those. I used AI
to help generate huge numbers of fixtures from the
upstream compiler,

749
01:05:00.171 --> 01:05:08.104
ubugeeei: then rewrote it in Rust to match those
fixtures exactly. [laughs]

750
01:05:08.104 --> 01:05:12.372
ubugeeei: Like that. [laughs] The fixtures are where the
two implementations meet.

751
01:05:12.372 --> 01:05:17.040
ubugeeei: I didn't port the TypeScript implementation
into Rust file by file,

752
01:05:17.722 --> 01:05:22.991
ubugeeei: Nor did I simply set out to write a Vue
compiler in Rust [exact premise unclear].

753
01:05:22.991 --> 01:05:28.100
ubugeeei: I generated an enormous number of fixtures in
advance and used those as the target,

754
01:05:28.100 --> 01:05:31.888
ubugeeei: then had it write Rust code from scratch to
meet that target. [laughs]

755
01:05:31.888 --> 01:05:33.412
ubugeeei: That's my approach.

756
01:05:33.412 --> 01:05:38.386
ubugeeei: It's interesting how different the process is
from rsvelte's.

757
01:05:38.386 --> 01:05:41.714
baseballyama: With rsvelte, it's easy to predict that,
for quite a long time,

758
01:05:42.298 --> 01:05:47.546
baseballyama: the JavaScript version will remain the
upstream implementation.

759
01:05:47.546 --> 01:05:50.030
baseballyama: When a change is made in JavaScript,

760
01:05:51.072 --> 01:05:57.842
baseballyama: for example, Svelte's compiler is roughly
divided into three steps: parsing, validation,

761
01:05:57.842 --> 01:05:59.725
baseballyama: and transformation.

762
01:05:59.725 --> 01:06:03.030
baseballyama: Sometimes it's a little ambiguous which
layer should handle something.

763
01:06:03.030 --> 01:06:07.497
baseballyama: Depending on the layer, you can get
different error behavior,

764
01:06:07.497 --> 01:06:11.984
baseballyama: or a case might compile successfully, even
if that's actually just a bug.

765
01:06:11.984 --> 01:06:14.808
baseballyama: Those differences can happen.

766
01:06:14.808 --> 01:06:19.677
baseballyama: So even when you're implementing the same
behavior, which layer does what

767
01:06:19.677 --> 01:06:21.741
baseballyama: can matter to some extent.

768
01:06:21.741 --> 01:06:25.729
baseballyama: If you can look at the JavaScript
implementation and say, 'This happens at this layer,

769
01:06:25.729 --> 01:06:28.415
baseballyama: and this happens at that layer,' then port
it as it is,

770
01:06:28.415 --> 01:06:33.986
baseballyama: those problems are less likely. That's why
I thought I'd try that approach.

771
01:06:33.986 --> 01:06:36.150
Theo: So it's a fairly faithful port?

772
01:06:37.351 --> 01:06:44.263
baseballyama: If you look at the major directory
divisions, I think Svelte's compiler has 1. parse,

773
01:06:44.263 --> 01:06:48.090
baseballyama: 2. validate, and 3. transform.

774
01:06:48.090 --> 01:06:54.621
baseballyama: But the contents are quite different, so
it isn't actually a faithful one-file-to-one-file port.

775
01:06:54.621 --> 01:06:58.889
baseballyama: Broadly speaking, though, that's the kind
of port it is.

776
01:06:58.527 --> 01:07:05.540
Theo: Rust and JavaScript are quite different, so I
wouldn't expect an entirely faithful port anyway.

777
01:07:05.540 --> 01:07:09.768
baseballyama: And there are libraries involved, like
esrap.

778
01:07:09.768 --> 01:07:13.515
baseballyama: Naturally, there isn't a Rust version of
esrap.

779
01:07:13.515 --> 01:07:17.303
baseballyama: In the parser, there are also places where
you use Oxc's parser,

780
01:07:17.303 --> 01:07:21.209
baseballyama: which differs a little from the JavaScript
ecosystem.

781
01:07:22.875 --> 01:07:29.583
baseballyama: The available libraries are quite
different too, so of course a completely faithful port
isn't possible.

782
01:07:29.583 --> 01:07:35.049
Theo: By the way, how do you parse the HTML, or rather,
the template part?

783
01:07:35.049 --> 01:07:39.494
baseballyama: I write the template parser myself.

784
01:07:35.700 --> 01:07:38.000
ubugeeei: Yourself. Me too.

785
01:07:39.795 --> 01:07:42.478
baseballyama: Do you have to do that yourself for Vue
too?

786
01:07:40.650 --> 01:07:41.750
ubugeeei: Huh? Which part?

787
01:07:42.538 --> 01:07:47.925
baseballyama: The template. In JavaScript, you can parse
Vue's HTML-like templates with parse5,

788
01:07:47.925 --> 01:07:51.074
baseballyama: but in Rust, is it better to write your
own?

789
01:07:51.335 --> 01:07:56.922
ubugeeei: Actually, with Vue too, when you think about
optimization,

790
01:07:56.922 --> 01:08:04.572
ubugeeei: it's better to use a Vue-specific parser than
a general-purpose one. The current implementation does
that as well.

791
01:08:05.433 --> 01:08:08.417
ubugeeei: There's a library called htmlparser2.

792
01:08:08.417 --> 01:08:15.586
ubugeeei: They forked it and extended it for
interpolation.

793
01:08:15.586 --> 01:08:18.503
ubugeeei: Using that forked, extended version

794
01:08:18.503 --> 01:08:26.259
ubugeeei: gives much better performance. So ultimately,
you end up doing it yourself.

795
01:08:26.259 --> 01:08:28.925
ubugeeei: That's what I'm doing too.

796
01:08:28.925 --> 01:08:32.614
ubugeeei: Also, going back to what we were saying,

797
01:08:32.614 --> 01:08:37.783
ubugeeei: one clear difference between my stance and
Yama's

798
01:08:38.024 --> 01:08:45.952
ubugeeei: is that I don't think the current upstream
Vue.js compiler is an especially good implementation.
[laughs]

799
01:08:45.952 --> 01:08:53.340
ubugeeei: How should I put this? There are concerns from
a language-implementation perspective.

800
01:08:53.340 --> 01:08:58.746
ubugeeei: If you ask whether it's a really well-built
language implementation, it isn't exactly that.

801
01:08:58.746 --> 01:09:03.070
ubugeeei: Some parts are quite ad hoc, more like
workarounds.

802
01:09:05.369 --> 01:09:09.635
ubugeeei: I wanted to bring those pieces together more
systematically [exact wording unclear].

803
01:09:09.635 --> 01:09:14.562
ubugeeei: and, how do I put it, do it properly. That's
an odd way to phrase it,

804
01:09:14.562 --> 01:09:20.991
ubugeeei: but I wanted to design the vertical
integration properly and build a really good language
implementation.

805
01:09:20.991 --> 01:09:27.900
ubugeeei: Since I'm not especially in love with the
upstream compiler in the first place, [laughs]

806
01:09:27.900 --> 01:09:31.330
ubugeeei: matching it isn't my goal. That's a big
difference in our approaches.

807
01:09:31.330 --> 01:09:38.222
ubugeeei: There are lots of cases where I think a
different architecture would make a new feature easier
to implement.

808
01:09:38.222 --> 01:09:45.595
ubugeeei: The clearest example is compiler macros.
Rather than interpreting them as compiler macros,

809
01:09:45.595 --> 01:09:49.101
ubugeeei: the implementation parses the JavaScript AST,

810
01:09:49.101 --> 01:09:54.124
ubugeeei: then does further processing in terms of
function calls [exact mechanics unclear].

811
01:09:54.124 --> 01:09:59.913
ubugeeei: But as a Vue concept, compiler macros should
really be evaluated as compiler macros.

812
01:09:59.913 --> 01:10:07.705
ubugeeei: It should have an IR. I wanted to change the
design itself from the outset.

813
01:10:07.705 --> 01:10:13.154
ubugeeei: That's a clear difference between my position
and Yama's.

814
01:10:13.820 --> 01:10:15.566
baseballyama: Maybe I shouldn't say this,

815
01:10:15.566 --> 01:10:22.371
baseballyama: but if we were still on Svelte 4, I might
have done the same thing. [laughs]

816
01:10:21.700 --> 01:10:23.050
ubugeeei: Ah. [laughs]

817
01:10:22.411 --> 01:10:23.735
baseballyama: Up through Svelte 4,

818
01:10:24.942 --> 01:10:29.330
baseballyama: I don't know whether it was exactly the
same codebase since Svelte 1.0,

819
01:10:29.751 --> 01:10:36.825
baseballyama: but I think Svelte was probably Rich
Harris's first time writing a compiler.

820
01:10:36.825 --> 01:10:40.894
baseballyama: He worked hard on it, and it was good,

821
01:10:40.894 --> 01:10:45.223
baseballyama: but things gradually accumulated over
time.

822
01:10:45.223 --> 01:10:51.648
baseballyama: We'd occasionally get issues saying the
source code was difficult to understand

823
01:10:51.648 --> 01:10:58.296
baseballyama: and hard to contribute to. Was it really
easy to follow?

824
01:10:58.296 --> 01:11:03.223
baseballyama: There were certainly times when you
couldn't wholeheartedly say yes.

825
01:11:03.223 --> 01:11:08.770
baseballyama: Svelte 5 involved Dominic Gannaway, who
used to be on the React team,

826
01:11:08.770 --> 01:11:14.120
baseballyama: created Inferno, and now works on things
like Ripple and Octane.

827
01:11:14.120 --> 01:11:16.000
baseballyama: TSRX. TSRX. [name unverified]

828
01:11:16.000 --> 01:11:19.000
Theo: He's at OpenAI now.

829
01:11:19.000 --> 01:11:25.500
baseballyama: He worked on a very broad range of the
implementation,

830
01:11:25.500 --> 01:11:31.450
baseballyama: He had a lot of experience with compilers
and JavaScript toolchains,

831
01:11:31.730 --> 01:11:33.953
baseballyama: and Svelte 5 was rebuilt from scratch.

832
01:11:33.953 --> 01:11:36.335
baseballyama: That made the code much easier to
understand.

833
01:11:36.335 --> 01:11:38.717
baseballyama: Svelte 5 also introduced Runes,

834
01:11:38.717 --> 01:11:43.222
baseballyama: which moved some of what Svelte 4 handled
in the compiler into the runtime.

835
01:11:43.222 --> 01:11:46.567
baseballyama: That reduced the complexity of the
compiler itself.

836
01:11:46.567 --> 01:11:52.717
baseballyama: So compared with Svelte 4, things became
much cleaner and better organized.

837
01:11:52.717 --> 01:11:55.121
baseballyama: That may partly explain my approach too.

838
01:11:55.622 --> 01:12:02.054
ubugeeei: Actually, to be fair, Vue was also rewritten
from v2 to v3,

839
01:12:02.054 --> 01:12:06.482
ubugeeei: and it's continually refactored. I wouldn't
say its code is hard to read.

840
01:12:06.482 --> 01:12:09.226
ubugeeei: The source code is properly designed.

841
01:12:09.226 --> 01:12:14.372
ubugeeei: Maybe it's more accurate to say it wasn't the
language implementation I wanted to write. [laughs]

842
01:12:14.372 --> 01:12:20.079
ubugeeei: Something like that. That sounded a bit
defensive,

843
01:12:20.079 --> 01:12:24.834
ubugeeei: but Vue's implementation is fine for what it
is.

844
01:12:24.994 --> 01:12:32.325
ubugeeei: It isn't terrible code or anything. It's
properly organized.

845
01:12:32.325 --> 01:12:38.795
ubugeeei: It's just that Vue.js core is really designed
only as a compiler.

846
01:12:38.795 --> 01:12:45.620
ubugeeei: When you think about the whole toolchain, or
Vue.js as a sort of programming language,

847
01:12:45.620 --> 01:12:52.410
ubugeeei: is it designed as a complete language
implementation, in the way Rust

848
01:12:52.410 --> 01:12:57.317
ubugeeei: and various other languages are? Not really.
Take the type checker too.

849
01:12:57.317 --> 01:13:03.286
ubugeeei: It has its own parser, or generates a separate
intermediate representation,

850
01:13:03.286 --> 01:13:07.956
ubugeeei: and manipulates things differently. I wanted
to unify all of that as one language.

851
01:13:07.956 --> 01:13:14.169
ubugeeei: So I started thinking about it from the design
stage. That's part of it.

852
01:13:14.169 --> 01:13:21.043
ubugeeei: Looking at Vue's compiler purely as a
compiler, I don't think there's anything particularly
wrong with the code.

853
01:13:21.043 --> 01:13:23.328
ubugeeei: That's roughly how I'd put it.

854
01:13:23.328 --> 01:13:27.437
Theo: I'm really glad I invited both of you today.

855
01:13:27.437 --> 01:13:33.392
Theo: We've discovered that your goals are fairly
similar, but your approaches are completely different.

856
01:13:33.392 --> 01:13:38.525
Theo: I skimmed the source code a little while
preparing,

857
01:13:38.525 --> 01:13:46.300
Theo: but I couldn't see all of that just from reading
it. I'm glad we deliberately compared the two.

858
01:13:46.300 --> 01:13:52.090
Theo: We've been going for over an hour now, so I'd like
to start wrapping up.

859
01:13:52.090 --> 01:13:58.500
Theo: I think you'll each be speaking about your
projects, so please tell us about your talks,

860
01:13:58.500 --> 01:14:02.927
Theo: and share any final thoughts you'd like to leave
us with.

861
01:14:03.906 --> 01:14:09.834
ubugeeei: We said we'd talk about Vue Fes, and then
hardly talked about it at all. [laughs]

862
01:14:09.914 --> 01:14:16.864
ubugeeei: What do I want to promote? Well, maybe nothing
in particular. [laughs]

863
01:14:16.864 --> 01:14:20.870
ubugeeei: I'm a speaker, but I'm also on the organizing
team,

864
01:14:20.870 --> 01:14:25.121
ubugeeei: so here's the official plug: if you haven't
bought a ticket yet, please do. [laughs]

865
01:14:25.121 --> 01:14:30.667
ubugeeei: Anything else for me? Yama, do you have
something?

866
01:14:30.667 --> 01:14:38.517
ubugeeei: You might have more to say as a speaker. I'm
mostly looking at it as an organizer. [laughs]

867
01:14:38.517 --> 01:14:45.627
baseballyama: Right. When I used to think of technical
events,

868
01:14:45.627 --> 01:14:52.077
baseballyama: I pictured going to a university

869
01:14:52.077 --> 01:14:57.546
baseballyama: and sitting in a classroom, like those
Google events.

870
01:14:57.546 --> 01:14:59.990
baseballyama: That was the sort of image I had.

871
01:14:59.990 --> 01:15:04.518
baseballyama: But Vue Fes has 'Fes' in its name.

872
01:15:04.518 --> 01:15:10.768
baseballyama: Listening to the talks is fun, of course,

873
01:15:10.768 --> 01:15:16.578
baseballyama: but the whole atmosphere gets you excited
too.

874
01:15:17.018 --> 01:15:22.988
baseballyama: It's a really enjoyable place. Especially
since last year or so, it hasn't just been about Vue,
but the ecosystem around Oxc.

875
01:15:22.988 --> 01:15:28.935
baseballyama: VoidZero was acquired and became part of
Cloudflare,

876
01:15:28.935 --> 01:15:32.603
baseballyama: and you get to hear about the cutting edge
of that ecosystem.

877
01:15:32.603 --> 01:15:39.760
baseballyama: You learn things, and it's simply a fun
place to be.

878
01:15:39.760 --> 01:15:45.090
baseballyama: So if you haven't bought a ticket... I'm
not an organizer or anything; they're just making me say
this. [laughs]

879
01:15:45.090 --> 01:15:49.650
baseballyama: Personally, I think buying a ticket will
be well worth your time.

880
01:15:49.950 --> 01:15:50.850
baseballyama: Yeah.

881
01:15:51.483 --> 01:15:55.290
ubugeeei: Thank you for pitching Vue Fes better than the
organizers. [laughs]

882
01:15:54.870 --> 01:16:01.861
Theo: I really think that's exactly right. It really is
a festival, and the talks are interesting.

883
01:16:01.861 --> 01:16:07.590
Theo: And beyond the talks, there's what we call the
hallway track in English.

884
01:16:07.590 --> 01:16:13.480
Theo: It literally means a track in the hallway. It
isn't unique to Vue Fes;

885
01:16:13.480 --> 01:16:18.709
Theo: conferences have this hallway track where you
simply talk to the people you meet,

886
01:16:18.709 --> 01:16:22.395
Theo: and learn new things through those conversations.

887
01:16:22.395 --> 01:16:29.526
Theo: Vue Fes brings together lots of people who are
passionate about this technology,

888
01:16:29.526 --> 01:16:35.820
Theo: so I'd really recommend going. And Yama, this
isn't related to Vue Fes,

889
01:16:35.820 --> 01:16:42.189
Theo: but I'm a bit of a Svelte fan myself, so I wanted
to give this a shout-out too:

890
01:16:42.189 --> 01:16:44.433
Theo: Svelte Summit.

891
01:16:44.433 --> 01:16:48.639
baseballyama: Right. It's on November 19 and 20, over
two days.

892
01:16:48.639 --> 01:16:54.388
baseballyama: Svelte Summit is in Ljubljana, Slovenia.
[laughs]

893
01:16:54.388 --> 01:16:59.634
baseballyama: It's not exactly somewhere you can
casually drop by. There's no direct flight, so you need
a connection.

894
01:16:59.634 --> 01:17:05.260
baseballyama: It takes a bit of effort to get there, but
Svelte Summit runs for two days there.

895
01:17:05.260 --> 01:17:11.206
baseballyama: I'm planning to give a talk about rsvelte
too.

896
01:17:11.206 --> 01:17:18.134
baseballyama: There'll probably be an online stream, so
you should be able to watch online.

897
01:17:18.134 --> 01:17:20.516
baseballyama: Of course, I think Rich Harris will be
there too,

898
01:17:21.262 --> 01:17:24.466
baseballyama: so there should be some great talks to
look forward to.

899
01:17:24.466 --> 01:17:31.176
baseballyama: And if you have the time and money, please
come to Ljubljana in person.

900
01:17:31.176 --> 01:17:34.258
baseballyama: We'd be happy to have more people from
Japan there.

901
01:17:32.700 --> 01:17:35.450
ubugeeei: Are you going there to give your talk in
person, Yama?

902
01:17:35.640 --> 01:17:38.242
baseballyama: Yes, I'm going in person.

903
01:17:38.242 --> 01:17:38.683
ubugeeei: Oh!

904
01:17:38.683 --> 01:17:43.568
Theo: I don't think I can make it this time, but good
luck, Yama. [laughs]

905
01:17:43.568 --> 01:17:46.311
baseballyama: [laughs] Thank you.

906
01:17:46.311 --> 01:17:48.012
Theo: And with that,

907
01:17:48.012 --> 01:17:53.017
Theo: we'll wrap up this episode of UIT INSIDE.
Everyone,

908
01:17:53.017 --> 01:17:53.918
Theo: thank you.

909
01:17:53.918 --> 01:17:55.299
ubugeeei / baseballyama: Thank you.

